Letters - a word processor that reads Microsoft Word files
==========================================================

!Letters, in the MoreApps directory on the hard disc, is the start of a
word processor for Microsoft Word documents (.docx files). This
version opens a Word document, shows its text with the formatting it
uses, lets you select text, type and delete text with undo and redo,
format it (bold, sizes, colours, alignment, indents, capitals and
small letters...) from the keys, a Format menu, a toolbar and a ruler
(section 5), copy the look of text with the format painter (section
5), set the spacing, the tab stops, the borders and the shading of
its paragraphs (section 6), see it larger or smaller, shows the
numbers and bullets of its lists and keeps them right as you edit,
makes new bulleted and numbered lists (section 7), puts in page and
section breaks, symbols, special characters, hyperlinks and
bookmarks (section 8), shows its pictures and lets you resize,
delete, describe, put in and copy them (section 9), shows its
tables with their lines, shading and merged cells, lets you type,
select and format in their cells, and makes tables and changes
their rows, columns and cells, their width, lines and shading,
and the edges of their columns and rows, copies and pastes them
and converts text to tables and back (section 10), counts its
words (section 3), shows it as pages, as they will print, and sets
up its pages: paper, margins, orientation and how each section
starts (section 11), shows its headers and footers and lets you
change them, with page numbers, dates and other fields worked out
for each page (section 12), sets text in newspaper columns, shows
and sets line numbers, and places floating pictures and tables on
their pages with the text flowing round them (section 13), shows it
in Web layout and as an outline of its headings (section 2), and
saves it, losing nothing: back to its file, under another name, or
as a copy.
Click its icon for a new document; recent files are on its menu. It
copies, cuts and pastes, and finds and replaces text (section 4);
printing comes in a later version (see section 20).

It is written in JavaScript, and it is all on the disc: you can read
it (and change it) in !JsEdit, like the programs in the Programming
guide.

