1) Starting
-----------

Click the hard disc icon, open the MoreApps directory, and
double-click !Letters. Its icon (a blue page with a white L) appears on
the right of the icon bar. You do not need to start it to open a
document: double-click a .docx file in any directory display and
!Letters starts and shows it.
This works on the hard disc and on a HostFS drive too, so a Word file
on your computer can be read straight from its own folder (see the
HostFS guide).

You can also drop a .docx file on !Letters' icon on the icon bar, or on
the window of a document that is already open.

  SELECT on the icon     a new, empty document (section 15)
  MENU on the icon       New, Recent, Info, Quit (section 15)

Only one !Letters runs. More documents open in the same one, each in a
window of its own. A document that is already open is not opened a
second time: its window comes to the front.


2) What you see
---------------

The window shows the text of the document in the fonts it asks for
(see section 17). Bold, italic, underlining, strike-through, colours,
highlighting, superscript and subscript, sizes, headings, indents,
line spacing and the space around paragraphs (section 6), centred,
right-aligned or justified paragraphs, and the
numbers and bullets of lists (section 7) are shown. Lines are
wrapped to the width of the text on the document's page (its paper
size less its margins and any gutter at the side, the extra margin
Word keeps for binding; A4 with 1-inch margins if the document does
not say), much as Word would, and the page's column is centred in
the window. Each section is shown at the width of its own page: a
landscape section later in a portrait document is wider, as it is
in Word (in Draft the white page is as wide as the widest section:
scroll sideways to see all of it if the window is narrower). In
Draft a section set out in two or more columns of text is shown as
one column, at the width of its first column; Print layout sets the
columns side by side on its pages (section 13). Documents saved as
Strict Open XML are
shown at their own page size too. In a justified paragraph every
line but the
last (and one ending where you pressed SHIFT-Enter) is spread to
both margins by widening the spaces between its words. If the
window is narrower than the page, scroll sideways to see the rest.

Info on the window's menu says what is in the document: how many
paragraphs (those inside tables too) and tables, how many other
things it holds that are not shown (pictures, links, footnotes,
comments, tables kept exactly as they were...), and the fonts used.
A document of eight paragraphs and one table says "8 paragraphs,
1 table".

Some things are shown in a simple way:

  Tables          drawn, with their lines, shading and merged
                  cells (section 10); a few kept exactly as they
                  were are a grey box: "[table - preserved, not
                  shown]"
  Pictures        PNG, JPEG and GIF pictures are drawn (section 9);
                  in Print layout a floating one is placed on its
                  page and the text flows round it (section 13)
  Other drawings  a grey box with a label, such as "Picture not
                  shown: EMF"; a chart, shape or text box is one too
  Links           blue, underlined text
  Footnotes       a small [...] box where the note is marked
  Fields          the result only: a page number shows as the number
                  of its page (section 12; View > Field codes shows
                  the codes)

A picture is one thing too: a click on it selects it, and handles
let you change its size (section 9).

Marks that Word keeps for itself are not shown at all: spelling and
grammar marks, bookmarks, the start and end of a comment, and the
codes of a field (unless View > Field codes is on). Text inserted
with tracked changes, or inside a content control, is shown in the
paragraph's plain formatting; deleted text is not shown.

These things are all still in the document, and in what you save.

A document usually opens in Print layout (section 11 says which view
a window opens in): its pages are drawn one below the other, each a
white sheet of the document's paper (A4, Letter or whatever it
says), with grey desk between them, as Word shows them (section 11
says how the pages are made). Draft, on the View menu,
shows the text on one long white page instead, at least a sheet of
paper tall. Either way a new or short document shows a whole page,
as in Word. Click in it and a red caret appears where you clicked
(in the empty part below the text: at the end of the text); you can
select text, and type (see section 3):

  SELECT click          puts the caret there
  SELECT drag           selects; drag past the top or bottom of the
                        window and it scrolls
  double-click          selects a word
  triple-click          selects the paragraph
  SHIFT-click, ADJUST   extends the selection to there
  ADJUST drag           the same, following the pointer
  MENU                  the menu (the selection stays as it is,
                        wherever on the window you click)

The keys move the caret, and select as they go if SHIFT is held:

  Left, Right           a character (an emoji or an accented letter
                        is one character); marks you cannot see,
                        such as a bookmark, go with the next
                        character, so every press moves the caret
  Up, Down              a line
  CTRL-Left, -Right     a word
  CTRL-Up, -Down        a paragraph
  Home, End (Copy)      the start or end of the line
  CTRL-Home, CTRL-End   the start or end of the document
  Page Up, Page Down    a window's height (the view moves too)
  CTRL-Page Down        the first line of the next page (Print
                        layout; in Draft, Page Down)
  CTRL-Page Up          the first line of the previous page (Print
                        layout; in Draft, Page Up)
  CTRL-A                selects everything (Select all, on the
                        Edit menu)
  Escape                ends a selection (the caret stays where
                        the selection's moving end was)

A web browser may keep CTRL-Page Down and CTRL-Page Up for itself
(to change its own tabs), so they never reach !Letters: use Next page
and Previous page on the Edit menu instead, which do the same.

The triple-click is a click that comes soon after a double-click
(within the double-click time), as in !Edit. In Print layout, a click
in a page's top or bottom margin puts the caret on the page's first
or last line, and a click in the grey between two pages on the
nearer page's edge line; a double-click in the band where a page's
header or footer goes (just above or below the text) edits it
(section 12), and a single click there does nothing. Up and
Down go from the last line of one page to the first of the next, and
a drag down past the window selects on across the pages. A drag
past the left or right edge of the window scrolls sideways as well.
The selection ends where the pointer is when you let go of the
button.

The caret goes into a table's cells: click in one, or move into it
with the arrows. A drag from one cell to another selects cells, not
text (section 10 has the keys and the mouse's cell, row and column
zones). Where a table has nothing beside it, the caret may also go
before or after the table, and a click on it there, CTRL-A or a drag
across it selects the whole table.

Text that is written right to left (Arabic, Hebrew) is shown, but
it is not handled as such yet: the caret and the selection move
through it in the order it is stored, left to right, so they may
not follow the way the text reads or looks on the screen. Typing
and deleting in it work on the stored order too.

Selected text can be copied, cut and pasted (section 4), but not
dragged yet. The keys not listed here or in sections 3 to 15 do
nothing (the desktop's own, F12 and the rest, still work).

The View item of the window's menu (after Insert) chooses how the
document is shown:

  Print layout      the pages, one below the other, as they will
                    print (the way most documents open)
  Web layout        the text as wide as the window (a little in
                    from each side), with no page edges, headers
                    or footers: drag the window's size box and the
                    lines break again to fit (a long document first
                    re-breaks what you can see and finishes the
                    rest in the background; you can type meanwhile)
  Outline           the document as an outline: each heading
                    indented a quarter inch for each level below
                    Heading 1, the text under a heading one step
                    further in, and a mark to the left of each
                    paragraph: - a heading with text under it, + a
                    heading whose text is folded away, o a heading
                    with nothing under it, a small o ordinary text
                    (tables and pictures count as text). Its keys
                    and its menu are below
  Draft             the text on one long page, without page edges:
                    the quickest view to type in
  Formatting marks  ticked while on: Print layout shows the page,
                    column and section breaks as labelled dotted
                    lines too (Draft always shows them)
  Ruler             shows or hides the ruler (section 5)
  Field codes       ticked while each field shows its code, such as
                    { PAGE }, in place of its result (SHIFT-F9 does
                    the same); choose it again for the results.
                    Only the display changes: the document, and the
                    file you save, stay exactly as they were
  Field shading     Never, When selected or Always: a light grey
                    behind field results. When selected (as it
                    starts) shades a field while the caret is in it
                    or next to it; the choice is kept for next time

Only Field codes has a key (F8 and F9 stay Undo and Redo, as in
!Edit).

In Outline these keys work on the outline:

  SHIFT-Tab           with the caret at the start of a heading:
                      one level up (Heading 3 becomes Heading 2);
                      at the start of ordinary text it becomes
                      Heading 1. Heading 1 stays
  Tab                 with the caret at the start of a heading:
                      one level down; Heading 9 becomes ordinary
                      text
  CTRL-SHIFT-Up       moves the paragraph the caret is in (or the
                      selected paragraphs) above the one before
  CTRL-SHIFT-Down     moves it below the one after

Anywhere else Tab and SHIFT-Tab do what they do in the other views:
Tab types a tab, in a list item they change its level, and in a
table's cell they go to the next or the cell before.

A heading whose text is folded away (+) moves with everything under
it, and moving past it jumps over all of it. A field (such as a
table of contents), a bookmark or a comment that runs from the
paragraph into others takes them with it, so it stays whole. A
paragraph never moves past a section break, or above the first
paragraph or below the last: the computer beeps and nothing
changes. Each one is undone
with CTRL-Z. If the document has no style for the new heading level,
Letters adds it (Heading 7, 8 and 9 look like Heading 4 to 6). In
the other views CTRL-SHIFT-Up and Down select a paragraph at a time,
as listed above.

In Outline the window menu also has an Outline entry (shaded in the
other views; it comes after View) with the same commands, for when
the keys are not at hand: on a Mac the system keeps CTRL-SHIFT-Up
and Down, so Move up and Move down are there to use.

  Expand              opens the heading the caret is in (or each
                      heading in the selection) so the text under
                      it shows again
  Collapse            folds away the text and headings under the
                      heading the caret is in (in ordinary text:
                      the heading above it). Its mark becomes +.
                      A heading with nothing under it stays as it
                      is, and the computer beeps
  Show level          Level 1 to Level 9 show the headings down to
                      that level and no ordinary text: Level 2 is
                      Heading 1 and 2 only. All shows everything
                      again, except the headings you folded by
                      hand. A caret that was in text now out of
                      sight moves to the heading above it. After
                      Show level, Expand shows one more level and
                      Collapse one less
  Show first line only  ticked while on: each paragraph shows only
                      its first line. The caret stays on that line:
                      Right at its end goes on to the next
                      paragraph, and what you type goes in where
                      the caret is drawn. Choose it again to see
                      whole paragraphs
  Promote, Demote     as SHIFT-Tab and Tab above, whatever the
                      caret's place in the paragraph. A heading
                      whose headings are folded away (by Collapse
                      or by Show level) takes them one level too
                      (the ordinary text under it stays as it is)
  Move up, Move down  as CTRL-SHIFT-Up and Down; the keys are shown
                      beside them

Double-click the + or - to the left of a heading to fold it away or
open it (a double-click on its text still selects a word). What is
folded away, the level shown and Show first line only belong to the
window: they change nothing in the document, are not saved in the
file, and stay when you change to another view and back.

The caret never goes into text that is folded away. If an edit, an
Undo or a change of view would leave it there, it moves to the end
of the heading above. Find, Replace all and Go to (Insert >
Bookmark...) open the headings over what they find, as Word does.
Return at the end of a heading whose text is folded away (+) starts
a new heading of the same level after that text. Backspace at the
start of the paragraph after folded text, and Delete at the end of
a + heading, only beep: they would join it to text you cannot see.

Update field and Update all fields, on the Edit menu (no keys: F9
stays Redo), put a field's value into the document itself. On the
screen a page number or a date is worked out as you look, but the
document keeps the result it was saved with until you update it.
Update field updates the field the caret is in or next to, or the
fields in the selection (in a header or footer you are editing: all
of its fields, with that page's number); Update all fields updates
every field of the text. Each is one change for Undo. A locked
field, or one !Letters cannot work out (a table of contents, a mail
merge field...), is left exactly as it is; when nothing can be
updated the computer beeps. Saving never updates a field.
The box at the right-hand end of the toolbar's second row says where
the caret is: 'Page 3 of 12' in Print layout (and '(Section 2)' when
there is more than one section); in Draft, Web layout and Outline,
only the section.
Section 11 has the rest: which view a window opens in, how the pages
are made, and how close they are to Word's.

Each page shows its header and footer, with its own page number
(section 12). A paragraph's line breaks are close to Word's but not
always the same, so a page may break in another place than in
Word.


3) Typing, deleting and undo
----------------------------

Click in the text to put the red caret where you want to type, and
type. What you type goes in at the caret, in the formatting of the
character before it. Anything that your keyboard can type works:
letters of any language, accents, symbols and emoji. Dead keys (type
an accent, then a letter) work as they do in other programs, and so
do the input methods for Japanese, Chinese, Korean and other
languages and the emoji picker of your computer or tablet: the text
being composed is drawn at the caret, underlined in blue, over
whatever follows it, and the line is laid out again when you commit
the text (until then the text after the caret may look overlapped).
Typing over a selection replaces it, in the formatting of the first
selected character.

  Enter                  a new paragraph (see below)
  SHIFT-Enter            a new line in the same paragraph
  Tab                    a tab; at the start of a bulleted or
                         numbered item, one level deeper (section 7)
  SHIFT-Tab              in a list item, one level higher
  Backspace              deletes the character before the caret
                         (an emoji or an accented letter is one
                         character)
  Delete                 deletes the character after the caret
  CTRL-Backspace         deletes the word before the caret
  CTRL-Delete            deletes up to the start of the next word
  Insert                 switches between inserting and
                         overwriting; the character after the
                         caret is shaded while it overwrites
  CTRL-Enter             a page break (section 8)
  CTRL-SHIFT-Enter       a column break (section 8); in a table,
                         splits the table (section 10)
  CTRL-SHIFT-Space       a no-break space (section 8)
  CTRL-SHIFT--           a non-breaking hyphen (section 8)
  SHIFT-F3               change case (section 5)
  CTRL-Z, F8             undo
  CTRL-Y, CTRL-SHIFT-Z   redo
  F9                     redo (F8 and F9 as in !Edit)
  SHIFT-F9               field codes shown or not (View > Field
                         codes); Update field, on the Edit menu,
                         has no key (section 12)
  CTRL-A                 selects everything
  CTRL-Page Down         the next page's first line (section 2; a
                         browser may keep it: Edit > Next page)
  CTRL-Page Up           the previous page's first line (Edit >
                         Previous page)

With text selected, Backspace and Delete (and the word forms) delete
the selection. At the start of a paragraph Backspace joins it to the
one before; at the end, Delete joins the next one to it. The
paragraph that comes first keeps its own look, unless it is empty
and the next one is not: then the empty one goes and the next keeps
its look. Enter in the middle of a paragraph splits it, and both
halves keep the paragraph's style; at its end, a new paragraph
follows in the style Word gives next (after a heading, normal text).
Enter in an empty bulleted or numbered paragraph ends the list.
Backspace at the start of a list item first takes its number or
bullet away; a second Backspace joins it to the paragraph before.
Typing * or 1. and a space at the start of a paragraph makes it a
list item (lists are in section 7).
Some things in a paragraph are never seen: bookmarks (section 8),
field codes, and text that Word has marked as deleted in a tracked
change. Left and Right pass such a mark together with the next
character, so a press always moves the caret on the screen, and
Backspace and Delete never remove one: they delete the character
beyond it and leave the mark where it was (to take a bookmark away,
use the Bookmark box; to deal with a tracked deletion, use Word).
An optional hyphen is not one of these: it is an ordinary
character to the keys, and Backspace removes it.
Typed tabs and new lines are kept in the text, and are saved as
Word's own tab and line break. A tab moves the text on to the
paragraph's next tab stop: the stops a Word document sets (left,
centred, right or on the decimal point, with their dotted or ruled
leaders) are followed, and where there are none, one every half
inch, counted from the left margin (or as the document says).
You can set tab stops of your own on the ruler (see "The ruler") or
in the Tabs box (section 6, "Tab stops").

Next to a table, Backspace and Delete follow Word: Backspace at the
start of the paragraph after a table moves the caret to the end of
the table's last cell (if that paragraph is empty, and not the last
in its place, it is removed first); Delete at the end of the
paragraph before a table moves nothing, except that an empty one is
removed and the caret goes to the table's first cell. An empty
paragraph between two tables is never removed, since two tables
that touch are one table to Word: Backspace moves the caret into
the table before it and Delete does nothing. A bookmark
whose end (or start) was in a paragraph removed this way is kept:
its mark goes where the caret goes. Backspace and Delete at the
start or end of a cell do nothing. Selected cells are
cleared or removed, not deleted by one keystroke: see section 10.
Typing at a table's edge starts a new paragraph beside it.

Undo and redo: the Edit item on MENU has Undo, Redo, Cut, Copy, Paste,
Select all, Find..., Find next, Find previous, Replace..., Next page
and Previous page (CTRL-Page Down and CTRL-Page Up, above), Update
field and Update all fields (section 12) and Word count... (the
numbers of words, characters, paragraphs and lines of
the selection or the document; it has no key: see "Word count"
at the end of this section), shown with their keys (cut, paste and
find are in section 4). Undo and Redo are shaded
when there is nothing to undo or redo. After an undo or redo the
caret is where the change was made. Each undo undoes one step:

  - a word you type, with the spaces typed after it, is one step; the
    next word is another step
  - Enter, Shift-Enter, Tab, and every deleting key are a step each
  - typing over a selection, and deleting a selection, are a step
  - a pause of a second in typing, or moving the caret (with the
    mouse or the keys), or Insert, starts a new step
  - a long run of typing is cut into steps of at most 128 keystrokes

!Letters keeps the last 1000 steps. Undoing all the way back leaves the
document as it was opened. A step made in a header or footer is
undone like any other: the window goes to the header or footer to
show it (section 12).

The title of the window shows a * after the name while the document
has changes that were not saved: undo back to the way it opened (or
was last saved) and the * goes. Save (section 14) removes it; saving
a copy does not, because the document itself is not saved by it.
Closing a window with changes asks first, and so does quitting
(section 15).

Some things are not what Word does, or not done yet:

  - You cannot type into a document that has no paragraph at all
    (the bell sounds).
  - Text typed just after a link is plain text after it; it does not
    become part of the link.
  - Formatting a selection that holds a link (or a field, a tracked
    change or a content control) changes the text around it; the
    link's own text keeps its look (section 5).
  - Deleting a range of text across a section break (where a Word
    document changes its page settings) does not remove the break:
    each section stays, and one that is emptied gets an empty
    paragraph. Word would join the sections. (Backspace at the start
    of a section and Delete at the end of one do remove the break:
    section 8.)
  - When a deletion leaves a section ending with a table, an empty
    paragraph is put after the table.
  - On a Mac, Cmd works as CTRL for Z, Y, A, S (save), N (new), F
    and G (find), K (hyperlink) and the format keys B, I, U, E and
    J (the menus show Cmd; the browser may keep Cmd-N for itself:
    use F2, section 15); Cmd-C, Cmd-X and Cmd-V copy, cut and paste
    (section 4). Cmd-R and Cmd-L do nothing in the document
    (the browser's reload and address bar are kept away from it):
    use CTRL-R and CTRL-L to align. Cmd-M is the Mac's own (it
    minimises the window): indent with CTRL-M. Other Cmd keys are
    the browser's.
  - Characters typed with AltGr or Option while the window does not
    have the keyboard focus can be lost: click in the document first
    (the red caret shows that it has the focus).

Word behaviours that are guessed, and still to be checked against
the real Word: the exact extent of CTRL-Backspace and CTRL-Delete
(and what they do at a new line); which paragraph's look wins when
two are joined; the style a paragraph has after Enter; joining
sections when deleting a range across a section break; where undo steps
begin and end; and where text typed after a link goes.


Word count
----------

Edit > Word count... (it has no key) opens a small window called Word
count with the numbers for the selected text, or, when nothing is
selected, for the whole document (the first line says which):

  Words                      Characters (no spaces)
  Characters (with spaces)   Paragraphs
  Lines

Close, RETURN or ESCAPE shuts the window and gives the caret back to
the document. Ask again (choose Word count... again, perhaps after
selecting something else) while it is open, and the numbers are
worked out again in the same window. Nothing in the document
changes: it is not a step for undo and the title gets no *.

How it counts:

  - A word is a run of characters with no white space in it:
    "well-known" and "3.5" are one word each. A no-break space
    (CTRL-SHIFT-Space) does not end a word, and is left out of
    "Characters (no spaces)" as a space is. Each Chinese, Japanese or
    Korean character counts as a word of its own, and ends the word
    beside it.
  - Characters are counted as you see them: a letter with an accent,
    or an emoji, is one; a tab or a new line (SHIFT-Enter) is one;
    the end of a paragraph is not counted, and neither is an optional
    hyphen.
  - Paragraphs are those with something in them: an empty paragraph
    is not counted. Lines are the lines the window shows for the
    selection or the document, the lines in a table's cells too;
    text in a table is counted in the words and characters.
  - What is counted is the text of the paragraphs, the text in a
    table's cells, the text of a link and what a field shows (a page
    number counts as the number). Not counted: field codes, text
    that a tracked change has deleted, text boxes, pictures,
    footnotes and endnotes, headers and footers.
  - A selection that holds a table counts the table's text. In a
    document with a very great deal of table text (over a million
    parts) the count stops and the window says that it is partial.

Word may count some things differently: each Asian character (Word
shows Asian characters and other words apart), an emoji (Word may
count two characters), and the no-break spaces. These are still to
be checked in real Word, as is whether Word's count with its box for
text boxes, footnotes and endnotes turned off gives the same numbers.


4) Copy, paste and find
-----------------------

Copy, cut and paste
-------------------

Select some text, then:

  CTRL-C                 copies it to the clipboard
  CTRL-X                 cuts it: copies it, then deletes it (one
                         undo step)
  CTRL-V                 pastes what is on the clipboard at the
                         caret, in place of any selection (one
                         undo step)

On a Mac use Cmd-C, Cmd-X and Cmd-V. The Edit item on MENU has Cut,
Copy and Paste too, with their keys (Cut and Copy are shaded while
nothing is selected). The clipboard is your computer's: text copied
in !Letters can be pasted into other programs, and text copied in them
can be pasted here. After a paste the caret is at the end of what
was pasted, and CTRL-Z takes the whole paste away again. A copy
changes nothing in the document (it adds no undo step and no *).

Between !Letters documents, and within one, a paste keeps the
formatting exactly: fonts, sizes, colours, bold and the rest, and
styles.

  - Within one document links, list numbering, pictures and tables
    come too (a pasted picture gets a number of its own, as Word
    gives it, and shares the picture file with the one copied).
    A table you copy comes as a table, with its cells, text and
    formatting, its pictures and links given numbers of their own.
    You can copy a whole table, some of its rows, or a rectangle
    of its cells (a cell merged across or down comes whole, so the
    rectangle grows to hold it), and a selection that runs from
    text into a table takes the rows it reaches along with the
    text. Section 10 says how a table is pasted. A bookmark, a
    comment or a tracked change in it stays with the original
    only, so that nothing that must be there only once is there
    twice. A table !Letters keeps exactly as it was (section 16) when
    it holds a bookmark, a comment, a tracked change or a picture
    comes as text instead (a paragraph for each row, its cells
    separated by tabs); a picture in it is not in the text, so the
    copy has no picture (the original keeps it).
    For the same reason the record of a tracked formatting change
    is not copied. Cutting a table and pasting it in the same
    document therefore loses its bookmarks and comments (the cut
    takes the original away); copy and paste leaves them on the
    original.
  - In another document a whole paragraph keeps its borders,
    shading, tab stops, indents, spacing and alignment, and a whole
    list item keeps its bullet or number: the list comes with it,
    as a new list of that document (pasted next to one of its
    lists, it does not join it). Whole means its end too: select up
    to the start of the next paragraph. A paragraph whose end is
    not selected (one item's text, or the last item when the
    selection stops at the end of its text) comes as text and takes
    the formatting of the paragraph it is pasted into; so does the
    first one pasted into the middle of a paragraph. The new list
    counts from its own start, so items copied from the middle of a
    list are numbered from 1 again (Restart and Set numbering value
    still work on them). Items from one list stay one list. A link
    becomes its text (no longer a link, and without the link's blue
    underlining), and a table comes as a table: its cells with their
    text, widths, lines and shading, its lists and pictures by the
    rules here (a table !Letters keeps exactly as it was is left out,
    and so is a content control in a cell).
    A paragraph style is used only if that document has one of the
    same name; a table style is copied into the document (section
    10).
  - Pictures come into another document with their picture files
    (a copy of each goes into that document), at the same size,
    with their crop and their alt text; CTRL-Z takes them and their
    files away again. A linked picture (its file kept
    outside the document), a picture that is also a link or has an
    SVG version or artistic effects, an old-style (VML) drawing, a
    chart or a shape is left out, as is a picture over 20 MB, and
    the pictures of one paste past the first 1000 or the first
    200 MB.
  - A copy made before a Revert counts as from another document.

What other programs get: the text (paragraphs, tabs and new lines
as they are), and its formatting as a web page has it: bold,
italic, underline, strikethrough, colours, sizes, fonts,
superscript and subscript, headings, lists, and links that lead to
a web or mail address. A table in the selection goes as a table
(a web page table with its merged cells, widths, lines and
shading) and, in the text, as a line for each row with the cells
separated by tabs (section 10); a table !Letters keeps exactly as it
was is an empty line.

What !Letters takes from other programs (a web page, Word, Google
Docs, a mail, Notepad): the text, its paragraphs and that same
formatting.

  - A heading takes this document's heading style of that level
    (Heading 1, Heading 2...), and is also made bold at Word's
    heading size.
  - List items come as ordinary paragraphs, without their bullets
    or numbers.
  - A table comes as a table (section 10): from a web page, Word,
    Google Docs, LibreOffice or a spreadsheet. One that is too big
    or too odd comes as text instead: a paragraph for each row, its
    cells separated by tabs.
  - A link comes as its text; pictures in the page do not come
    (a picture file alone on the clipboard does: see below).
  - Nothing in what is pasted is ever run or fetched: scripts,
    pictures, styles and the like are left out.

Some pastes do nothing:

  - Pasting only files (copied in a file manager, say) pastes
    nothing: the bell sounds and a message says that only PNG,
    JPEG and GIF pictures can be pasted. A pasted PNG, JPEG or
    GIF picture file, with no text beside it, is put in at the
    caret as a picture (replacing a selection), as Insert >
    Picture... does (section 9); a copy that has text as well (a
    spreadsheet's cells with their picture, say) pastes the text.
  - Pasting or cutting while an input method is still composing
    does nothing (the bell sounds): finish the composition first.

Paste on the menu asks the browser to read the clipboard: the
browser may ask you to allow it. If it will not, !Letters tells you to
press CTRL-V (Cmd-V) instead.

A large copy, whose formatting would take over about two million
characters, reaches other programs as its text only (as plain text,
and as a web page holding just that text); pasted in !Letters it still
comes whole, with its formatting. A copy of more than about two
million characters of text reaches other programs, and !Letters, as
plain text only. A paste of plain text brings at most 100,000
characters: if the clipboard held more, the rest is left out, the
bell sounds and a message says so.

Find and replace
----------------

  CTRL-F or F4           opens the Find box (Find... on the Edit
                         item of MENU does the same)
  CTRL-H                 opens it with the caret in Replace
                         (Replace... on the Edit menu)
  CTRL-G                 finds the next place again (Find next)
  CTRL-SHIFT-G           finds the place before (Find previous)

On a Mac use Cmd-F, Cmd-G and Cmd-Shift-G; Replace stays CTRL-H
(Cmd-H hides the program on a Mac). If a few words on one line are
selected, they are put in Find for you.

Type what to look for in Find, then press RETURN (in either field)
or click Find next: the next place after the selection is selected,
its window comes to the front and scrolls to show it, and the box
moves out of the way if it was over it. Find previous looks back
from the selection. At the end of the document the search goes on
from the top (from the bottom looking back), and the line at the
foot of the box says so. If the text is nowhere, the bell sounds
and the box says Not found. CTRL-G that goes round, or finds
nothing, with the box closed opens the box to say so (the caret
stays in the document).

  Match case     capitals and small letters must be as typed
  Whole words    'cat' does not find 'concat' or 'cats' (a space
                 or a mark such as . or , at either end of what
                 you type needs no word edge there: 'cat,' is
                 found in 'cat, dog')

Type the new text in Replace, then:

  Replace        if the selection is what Find holds, it is replaced
                 (one step for CTRL-Z) and the next one is found;
                 otherwise this only finds the next
  Replace all    replaces it everywhere in the document at once,
                 and says how many: it is one undo step, so one
                 CTRL-Z puts them all back

The new text takes the formatting of the first character it replaces.
In the box's fields CTRL-V (Cmd-V on a Mac) pastes the first line of
the clipboard; CTRL-C and CTRL-X do nothing there (a field has no
selection to copy). Escape, Close or the box's close icon close the
box and give the caret back to the document. The box keeps what you
typed for next time. There is one Find box: it works on the document
you last clicked in, so you can go from window to window with it open.

  - The text is found as typed: there are no wildcards or patterns
    ('a.*b' finds just that, and so do ? * [ ] ( ) and \). A search
    never runs on from one paragraph into the next. Text in tables
    is searched, cell by cell and nested tables too, in the order
    you read it (a table's rows top to bottom, each row's cells left
    to right); Replace and Replace all change it as anywhere.
  - Text in a link is found (the whole link is selected, once
    however often it holds the text), but Replace and Replace all
    leave it as it is (Replace all says how many it left).
  - After Replace all the caret is after the last text put in.
  - An accented letter is found only as the same letter: e does not
    find an e with an accent.
  - Find holds at most 1000 characters.

Not possible yet
----------------

You cannot yet: drag selected text to move or copy it; paste files
other than pictures; keep a list's bullets or numbers when pasting
from another program; keep a link
a link in another document; paste as
plain text only (Word's Paste Special); find text in footnotes, or
in headers and footers from the body (in the header and footer mode
Find looks in that header only: section 12), or find formatting; or
replace the text of a link.


5) Formatting
-------------

Select text and press a key, choose from the Format item on MENU, or
click a button in the toolbar: all three do the same things. Each
change is one step that CTRL-Z undoes. A character format (bold, a
size, a colour...) changes the selected text; a paragraph format
(alignment, indent, style) changes every paragraph the selection
touches, or the one with the caret if nothing is selected. A format
you ask for that the text has already changes nothing.

The keys (the names are Word's; on a Mac Cmd works as CTRL for B,
I, U, E and J; the others need CTRL):

  CTRL-B                bold
  CTRL-I                italic
  CTRL-U                underline
  CTRL-=                subscript
  CTRL-SHIFT-=          superscript
  CTRL-SHIFT->          bigger: the next size up (8 9 10 11 12 14
                        16 18 20 22 24 26 28 36 48 72, then by
                        tens to 400)
  CTRL-SHIFT-<          smaller: the next size down
  CTRL-Space            clear the character formatting
  CTRL-L, CTRL-E        align the paragraph left, or centre it
  CTRL-R, CTRL-J        align it right, or justify it
  CTRL-M                indent the paragraph more (half an inch)
  CTRL-SHIFT-M          indent it less
  CTRL-SHIFT-L          a bulleted list, or take the bullets away
                        (section 7)
  SHIFT-F3              change case: capitals, small letters, then
                        a capital for each word (below)

Bold, italic, underline, superscript and subscript are switches: if
all the selected text has the format already, the key takes it off;
otherwise it puts it on all of it (so a selection that is partly
bold becomes all bold, as in Word). There is no key for
strikethrough, the font, the colours, the highlight or the borders:
use the menu or the toolbar. CTRL-Space takes off the font, size, bold,
italic, underline, strikethrough, colour, highlight, superscript or
subscript and character style of the selection; alignment, indents
and the paragraph's style stay.


The Format menu
---------------

Format, on MENU over the document, has:

  Bold, Italic, Underline, Strikethrough, Superscript, Subscript
  Font >        the fonts of the document, then Word's, then the
                desktop's under Desktop fonts >
  Size >        8 to 72 points, Bigger, Smaller
  Colour >      the 16 colours of the desktop, Automatic, and More
                colours...: forty swatches (Word's standard colours
                and shades), or any colour typed as six hex digits
                RRGGBB followed by RETURN
  Highlight >   None, or one of Word's 16 highlight colours
  Change case > Sentence case, lowercase, UPPERCASE, Capitalize Each
                Word, tOGGLE cASE, Next case (SHIFT-F3: see "Changing
                case" below)
  Align >       Left, Centre, Right, Justify
  Indent >      More, Less
  Line spacing > 1.0 to 3.0 lines, space before, space after
                (section 6)
  List >        Bullets >, Numbering >, Restart at 1, Continue
                numbering, Set numbering value..., Demote, Promote,
                Remove from list (section 7)
  Style >       the paragraph styles of the document
  Paragraph...  the Paragraph box (section 6)
  Tabs...       the Tabs box (section 6)
  Borders and shading...  lines round paragraphs, and a colour
                behind them or behind text (section 6)
  Picture...    the Picture box, for a selected picture (section 9)
  Page setup... the paper, orientation and margins (section 11)
  Layout...     how a section starts, its header and footer
                distances and vertical alignment (section 11)
  Columns...    how many columns of text, how wide, and a line
                between them (section 13)
  Clear formatting
  Format painter  copies the look of some text to other text (see
                "The format painter" below); no key
  AutoFormat lists  ticked while typing * or 1. and a space makes
                list items; click to turn it off or on (section 7)

A tick shows what the selection has (nothing is ticked when it has
a mixture), and the keys are shown beside the items. The menu is
shaded only in a document with no paragraph to format.

The colours in Format > Colour are the desktop's, so its Red is a
little darker than Word's red; the swatches in the toolbar and in
More colours... are Word's own colours (Red is FF0000). Both are
saved as the colour's six hex digits, so Word shows what you chose.
Automatic is the colour the paragraph's style gives (black, if it
gives none).


Formatting at the caret
-----------------------

With no text selected, a character format applies to what you type
next: CTRL-B, then type, and the new text is bold; CTRL-B again and
the text after it is plain. The menu and the toolbar show it (the B
is pressed in). It is forgotten when you move the caret (with the
mouse or the keys), undo or redo, or use another command that is not
a format. Pressing RETURN also forgets it: in Word the next
paragraph would still be bold, in !Letters it is not. Text typed without
a format of its own goes in the formatting of the character before
it, as always (section 3).


Changing case
-------------

Format > Change case > changes the capitals and small letters of the
selected text, or, with no text selected, of the word the caret is in
or at the end of (between spaces, or in an empty paragraph, nothing
happens):

  Sentence case          a capital for the first letter of each
                         sentence (after a full stop, ! or ?, and at
                         the start of the paragraph), the rest small
  lowercase              all small letters
  UPPERCASE              all capitals
  Capitalize Each Word   a capital for the first letter of each
                         word, the rest of it small
  tOGGLE cASE            capitals become small and small ones capitals
  Next case              the same as SHIFT-F3

SHIFT-F3 goes round the three forms in turn: UPPERCASE, then
lowercase, then Capitalize Each Word, then UPPERCASE again. Text that
is none of these (a sentence, or a mixture) goes to UPPERCASE first.
A step that would change nothing (a word like 3rd has no capitalized
form) is passed over, so the key always does something when the text
has any letters. Plain F3 is still Save as (section 14).

Each change is one step that CTRL-Z undoes, and nothing is a step if
nothing changed. The text keeps its fonts, bold and the rest, and the
selection and the caret stay where they were. The text never gets
longer or shorter, so a letter whose capital is two letters (the
German sharp s, or a ligature like fi) is left as it is; real Word
makes SS of it. A Greek capital sigma at the end of a word becomes
the final sigma when it is made small. The menu has no toolbar
button and no box.


The format painter
------------------

The format painter copies the look of some text to other text. Its
button is the last on the toolbar's second row; Format > Format
painter does the same (neither has a key). To use it:

  1. Put the caret in the text whose look you want, or select a
     piece of it.
  2. Click the button with SELECT. It stays pressed in (and the
     menu item is ticked) until the next step.
  3. Click in the text to be changed, or drag across it. The look
     is put on when you let the mouse button go, and the button
     comes up.

Click the button with ADJUST (or SHIFT with SELECT) instead and it
stays down for as many clicks and drags as you like, until you press
ESCAPE (which then does nothing else) or click the button again.
Clicking the button when it is down turns the painter off. The
pointer keeps its ordinary shape: look at the button to see whether
the painter is on.

What is picked up depends on what you had selected:

  - with the caret only: the character format the text typed there
    would get (bold, size, colour...), and the format of the
    paragraph (alignment, indents, spacing, tab stops, borders and
    shading, its style and its bullets or numbers)
  - with a selection inside one paragraph: the character format of
    its first character only
  - with a selection that reaches the end of a paragraph, or goes on
    into the next: the character format of its first character and
    also the format of the paragraph

What is put on: a drag gives the characters you dragged over, and
the paragraphs they touch when a paragraph format was picked up. A
click gives the word you clicked in (and its paragraph, again only
if a paragraph format was picked up). The characters get the picked
format exactly: whatever they had that the picked text does not
have is taken away (a bold word painted from plain text is plain
afterwards). A paragraph given a list format joins that list; a
paragraph given a plain format comes out of its list. Everything
painted is one step that CTRL-Z undoes, and if it would change
nothing it is no step.

If the numbering or style that was picked up is gone (you undid its
making while the painter was still down), that part is left out of
the painting and the rest goes on. Things that !Letters does not
change itself (a paragraph's text direction or frame, for one) stay
as the target has them. Whether Word does the same with a click, with
the character style of the picked text and with those things is
still to be checked.


Styles
------

Style on the Format menu, and the Style box of the toolbar, list the
paragraph styles the document has (Normal, Heading 1...); choose one
to give it to the paragraphs. Formatting you gave a paragraph or its
text stays on top of the style. You can only use styles that the
document already has: there is no way to make, change or delete a
style yet, and character styles are not offered.


The toolbar
-----------

The toolbar across the top of the window has the same things as
buttons, left to right:

  Style      the paragraph's style; click its arrow for the others
  Font       the font; its arrow lists the document's fonts, Word's
             and (in a submenu) the desktop's. Or click in it, type
             a name and press RETURN
  Size       the size in points; its arrow lists Word's sizes. Or
             type one (1 to 400, halves allowed) and press RETURN;
             ESCAPE puts back what was there
  up, down   one size bigger or smaller
  B I U S    bold, italic, underline, strikethrough
  x2 x2      superscript, subscript
  A          the text colour, shown in the bar under the A: click
             for the swatches, Automatic and the hex field (at
             Automatic the style's colour is used)
  marker     a highlight colour behind the text (a list of names)
  lines      align left, centre, right, or justify
  arrows     indent less or more (half an inch)
  A x        clear the character formatting

A second row under these has the Line spacing button (section 6),
and the Bullets and Numbering buttons, each with a small arrow that
opens its gallery of bullets or numberings (section 7), and, last,
the Format painter button (below).

A button is pressed in when all of the selection has that format.
When the selection has a mixture (two fonts, two sizes, text partly
bold, paragraphs aligned differently) the font and size boxes are
empty and the buttons are out, none of the four alignments is
pressed in, and the colour bar is blank. With the caret only, they
show the format of the text you would type.

Clicking a button leaves the caret and the selection where they
were, so you can carry on typing; after RETURN in a box the caret
goes back to the text. In a narrow window the buttons that do not
fit are cut off at the right: widen the window, or use the keys or
the Format menu. With Help running, point at a button to see what
it does. MENU over the toolbar gives the window's menu.


The ruler
---------

Under the toolbar is a ruler, in inches from the left margin (the
margins are grey; as in Word, the inches are counted out from the
left margin both ways, 1 2 3... to the right and 1 to the left). It
shows the indents of the paragraph with the caret (with a
selection, its first paragraph), with four markers:

  top triangle     where the first line starts
  bottom triangle  where the other lines start (the hanging indent)
  box under it     the left indent
  right triangle   the right indent

Drag one with SELECT (a dotted line shows where it is):

  box              moves the left indent, and the first line with
                   it, so the paragraph keeps its shape
  bottom triangle  moves where the other lines start; the first
                   line stays where it is
  top triangle     moves the first line only; drag it left of the
                   bottom triangle for a hanging indent
  right triangle   moves the right indent

A marker moves in steps of 1/16 inch; hold SHIFT to move it freely.
The text always keeps a quarter of an inch between the indents.
When you let go, every paragraph in the selection gets the indent
the marker was left at (each keeps what you did not move), as one
undo step. If you let go with the pointer outside the window, it is
set where the pointer was.

The ruler also shows the paragraph's tab stops: black ones are the
paragraph's own, grey ones come from its style. Click SELECT on the
ruler, between the margins and away from the markers, to add a tab
stop there, of the kind shown in the box at the ruler's left end
(click the box for the next kind: left, centre, right, decimal). It
goes to the nearest 1/16 inch; hold SHIFT to place it exactly. Drag
a tab stop to move it (SHIFT: freely); drag it down off the ruler to
remove it. Where a tab stop and an indent marker are at the same
place, press on the lower part of the ruler to take the tab stop and
higher up to take the triangle. Like the indents, this changes every
selected paragraph, as one undo step.

The computer beeps and nothing changes when a paragraph cannot take
the change: when it already has more than 64 tab stops of its own,
or when its tab stops are of a kind !Letters keeps exactly as the file
has them without changing them (more than 256 stops, or settings
written by a newer Word); change those in Word. Section 6 ("Tab
stops") says more about tab stops and the Tabs box.

The margins are shown but cannot be dragged: change them with
Format > Page setup... (section 11). In Print layout the ruler
shows the page the caret is on. Choose Ruler on the View menu to
hide it (and again to show it); each window remembers this until
it is closed.


Zoom
----

To see the page larger or smaller, choose Zoom on the window's menu
(click MENU over the document): 50%, 75%, 100%, 125%, 150% or 200%,
or Zoom in and Zoom out for the next step. Holding CTRL and turning
the mouse wheel does the same (the wheel alone still scrolls). There
are no keys for zoom (CTRL-= and CTRL-SHIFT-= are subscript and
superscript), and pinching on a trackpad does not zoom: it scrolls.
The toolbar stays the same size (it has no zoom button); the ruler
is scaled with the page. Only the view changes: the text is laid out
as at 100%, so lines break in the same places at every zoom, and the
place you were looking at stays in the middle. Each window has its
own zoom, and it is not saved with the document.


What Word may show differently
------------------------------

!Letters does not have Word's own fonts or line breaking, so a
formatted document can look a little different in Word:

  - A font that this computer does not have is shown in Liberation
    Sans (section 17), but the document keeps the name you chose, and
    Word shows that font.
  - In a justified paragraph !Letters breaks the lines and then spreads
    each one, so a line may end at a different word in Word.
  - Superscript and subscript are drawn at about two thirds of the
    size, raised or lowered by part of it; Word's are a little
    different.
  - Text in the Automatic colour is drawn black, even on a dark
    highlight; Word may draw it white there.
  - Text is drawn at most 200 points high, though a size up to 400
    points can be chosen and saved: Word shows it at its full size.
  - A document that gives no size anywhere is shown in 11 points;
    a size you choose there is always saved, so Word shows what you
    chose.

Links, fields, tracked changes and content controls keep their own
look: formatting a selection that includes one changes the text
around it, and not the link's (or the field's) own text. Word would
change that text too.

These things are guessed, and still to be checked in real Word: the
way a partly bold selection toggles; un-bolding a heading; how much
CTRL-Space clears; the size and height of superscript; the colours
of the highlights; justified line breaks; a first line against a
hanging indent in the same paragraph; the Automatic colour; a
first-line marker dragged over paragraphs with different indents;
the snapping of the ruler; the format painter (what a click paints,
and whether it replaces the old format as !Letters does); and the size
that Word shows in a document that gives none.


Not possible yet
----------------

You cannot yet: make a list with bullets or numbers of your own
(you choose from the galleries: section 7); make or change a style;
format a paragraph that is empty (there is nothing in it to format;
Word would format its paragraph mark); or change footnotes.
(Headers and footers are formatted as the text is: section 12.)


6) Paragraphs: spacing, tabs, borders and shading
-------------------------------------------------

The space inside and between paragraphs is part of how a Word
document looks, and !Letters now both shows it and lets you change it:
the distance between the lines (line spacing), the space before and
after a paragraph, and, in the Paragraph box, the options that say
where a paragraph may be split across pages; then the tab stops
that line up columns of text; and lines round paragraphs and the
colour behind them. As with formatting
(section 5) each change is one step that CTRL-Z undoes, and it
applies to every paragraph the selection touches, or the one with
the caret if nothing is selected.

What you see
------------

!Letters draws the spacing a document asks for. A paragraph set to
double spacing now has twice the distance between its lines; a line
spacing of 'exactly 12 points' really is 12 points from one line to
the next (taller text is cut off at the line); the space
before and after paragraphs is left empty between them; and a
paragraph style that says 'don't add space between paragraphs of the
same style' (Word's List Paragraph does) has none between two of its
paragraphs. A document you open may therefore look different from
how earlier versions showed it: before, every line was drawn the
same distance apart, whatever the document said. Normal in a new
document is shown as Word makes it: 1.08 lines and 8 points after.
Nothing in the file is changed by seeing it differently.

Space that a web page put in with 'automatic' spacing (documents
made from HTML have it) is drawn as 14 points; this is a guess.

Line spacing: the keys and the toolbar
--------------------------------------

Word's keys (on a Mac too it is CTRL, not Cmd, for these):

  CTRL-1                single line spacing
  CTRL-5                one and a half lines
  CTRL-2                double
  CTRL-0                12 points of space before the paragraph, or
                        none if it has some already (with several
                        paragraphs selected: none, unless none of
                        them has any, then 12 points on all)

The toolbar has a second row under the first: Line spacing (an up
and down arrow beside some lines), Bullets and Numbering (each with
a small arrow for its menu: section 7) and the Format painter
(section 5). Click Line spacing and the same Line spacing menu opens
beside it; the caret stays in the document so you can carry on
typing.

The Line spacing item of the Format menu has:

  1.0, 1.15, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0     the line spacing in lines
  Add space before                  12 points above (CTRL-0)
  Remove space before
  Add space after                   12 points below
  Remove space after
  Line spacing options...           the Paragraph box (below)

The line spacing of the selection is ticked (nothing is when it has
a mixture, or a spacing that is not one of these: 'exactly', say,
which only the Paragraph box sets). Add space is shaded when the
paragraphs already have some, and Remove when they have none.

The Paragraph box
-----------------

Choose Paragraph... on the Format menu (or Line spacing options...
on the Line spacing menu, or in the toolbar's menu) to set several
things at once. The box shows the values of the paragraph with the
caret; if you selected several paragraphs that differ in something,
that field is empty (an option is off), and leaving it alone leaves
each paragraph as it is.

  Alignment        Left, Centre, Right or Justify
  Left, Right      the indents, in inches (0.5 or 0.5" or 36 pt)
  Special, By      (none), First line or Hanging, and by how much
  Before, After    the space above and below, in points (12 or
                   6.5 pt)
  Line spacing     Single, 1.5 lines, Double, At least, Exactly or
                   Multiple
  At               for Multiple the number of lines (1.15), for At
                   least and Exactly the height in points; shaded
                   for Single, 1.5 lines and Double
  Don't add space between paragraphs of the same style
  Line and page breaks:
    Widow/orphan control, Keep with next, Keep lines together,
    Page break before, Suppress line numbers

Click OK (or press RETURN) to apply what you changed, as one undo
step; only the fields you changed are applied. Cancel, ESCAPE or the
close icon change nothing. If a field holds something that is not a
length or number, or At is outside what !Letters can draw (Multiple 0.5
to 4 lines, At least and Exactly 1.5 to 1584 points; Word itself
allows up to 132 lines), the bell sounds and the box stays open with
the caret in the field. There is one Paragraph box for each
document; if you change the selection while it is open, OK refreshes
the box (with the bell) instead of applying a change to other
paragraphs than the ones it showed.

Line and page breaks
--------------------

Widow/orphan control, Keep with next, Keep lines together and Page
break before are saved in the file, exactly as Word saves them, and
in Print layout they decide where the pages break, as in Word
(section 11 says how). Page break before is also marked on the
screen in Draft, or with View > Formatting marks (a dotted line
above the paragraph: section 8); the other three show no mark. A
paragraph with no setting for
Widow/orphan control counts as having it on, as in Word, and
switching it off writes 'not on', which switching it on again takes
away.

Suppress line numbers leaves the paragraph out of the line numbers
that Print layout shows when the page's section numbers its lines
(section 13): its lines get no number, and the numbering goes on
after it. It is saved as Word saves it.

Tab stops
---------

A tab stop is a place across the page that a Tab in the text moves
to. A document can have stops of five kinds, and !Letters draws them,
lets you set them and saves them as Word does:

  Left      the text after the Tab starts at the stop
  Centre    the text after the Tab is centred on the stop
  Right     the text after the Tab ends at the stop
  Decimal   the text after the Tab has its first "." at the stop
            (with no "." it ends at the stop, as a right stop does)
  Bar       a thin vertical line drawn at the stop on every line of
            the paragraph (the Tab does not stop there)

Each stop except the bar can have a leader, which fills the space
that the Tab leaves with dots, dashes, a line (or a thick line) or
middle dots: the dotted line between a title and its page number in
a list of contents. Stops are counted from the left margin, not from
the indent, and a stop belongs to the paragraph (or its style) that
has it. Like the other paragraph settings, a change applies to every
paragraph the selection touches, as one undo step.

Where the stops come from: the paragraph's style may give some (they
are grey on the ruler), and the paragraph may add its own (black).
Your own stop at the same place as one of the style's replaces it.
Taking away a stop that the style gives is saved, as Word saves it,
as a "cleared" stop in the paragraph, so the style itself is not
changed.

The ruler's tab stops
---------------------

At the left end of the ruler is a small box showing a kind of stop.
Click it to step through the kinds: left, centre, right, decimal and
back to left; nothing in the document changes. Then:

  click SELECT on the ruler        adds a stop of that kind. Click
                                   between the margins and away
                                   from the indent markers
  drag a stop                      moves it, in steps of 1/16 inch
  SHIFT as you click or drag       places it exactly, not in steps
  drag a stop down off the ruler   removes it (it is shown dotted
                                   while you drag; let go off the
                                   ruler and it goes)

A stop from the paragraph's style is grey; dragging it off the ruler
clears it for this paragraph only, and dragging it along the ruler
makes it a stop of the paragraph's own at the new place and clears
the style's. Where a stop and an indent marker are at the same
place, press on the lower part of the ruler for the stop and higher
up for the triangle. If you select several paragraphs the ruler
shows the stops of the first, and what you do is done to each.

The ruler also marks the default tab stops (see below) as small grey
ticks along its foot, past the last stop of your own.

The computer beeps and nothing changes when a paragraph cannot take
the change. There are two reasons. A paragraph may already have more
than 64 tab stops of its own. Or its stops may be of a kind !Letters
cannot hold in full: a file written with more than 256 stops in one
paragraph, or with settings from a newer Word, is shown (the first 64
stops) but kept exactly as the file has it, so that nothing is lost
by opening and saving it. !Letters will not edit those stops, so it does
not risk spoiling them: change them in Word.

The Tabs box
------------

Choose Tabs... on the Format menu (or press the Tabs... button in the
Paragraph box) to set stops with numbers, to give them leaders, to
make a bar, and to change the default tab stop. The box has:

  Default tab stops   the distance between the tab stops that
                      nobody set, in inches (0.5, 0.5" or 36 pt);
                      the whole document's, not the paragraph's
  Tab stop position   where the stop is, in inches
  Stops               a list of the stops the paragraph has now:
                      pick one to see (and change) it
  Alignment           Left, Centre, Right, Decimal or Bar
  Leader              None, Dots, Dashes or Line
  Set                 puts the position, alignment and leader in the
                      list (a stop at the same place is replaced)
  Clear               takes the stop at the position out of the list
  Clear all           empties the list, so the paragraph keeps none
                      (a stop of its style is cleared as well)
  To be cleared       says what Clear and Clear all will remove

Nothing in the document changes until you click OK (or press RETURN).
Then the stops, and a new default tab stop if you typed one, are
applied together as one undo step. If the Tab stop position field
holds something when you click OK, that stop is set as well, as in
Word. Cancel, ESCAPE or the close icon change nothing. The box shows
the first paragraph of a selection, and OK does the same to every
paragraph in it. The bell sounds, and the box stays open, for a
position that is not a length or is more than 22 inches, a Clear
where there is no stop, a default tab stop under a fortieth of an
inch (or over 22 inches), a paragraph with more than 64 stops, and
the paragraphs with stops !Letters keeps exactly as the file has them
(see above); the default tab stop alone is still set for those. If
you change the selection or the text while the box is open, OK
refreshes the box (with the bell) instead of changing other
paragraphs than the ones it showed.

The default tab stop is saved in the document (Word's "default tab
stop" setting) and is used for every paragraph. A document with no
such setting uses half an inch, and !Letters does not add the setting
to a document that has none: the box shows 0.5" greyed. Changing it
moves every default tab on the screen and the ruler's grey ticks, and
CTRL-Z puts it back.

How tabs lay out
----------------

  - A Tab moves the text to the next stop to its right: the next
    stop of the paragraph if there is one, otherwise the next
    default stop, counted from the left margin. A stop of your own
    clears the default stops to its left, as in Word.
  - On the first line of a paragraph with a hanging indent, the
    indent counts as a stop: text before it goes to it. A glossary
    with the terms hanging at the left and their meanings at the
    indent therefore needs only one Tab. The default stops before a
    hanging indent are not used.
  - With a Tab in the middle of a line, a centre, right or decimal
    stop lines up the text up to the next Tab (or the end of the
    line). A price list on a decimal stop lines the points up. The
    spaces you type after right-aligned or centred text do not
    count: the text itself ends at (or is centred on) the stop.
  - When the text before the stop is too wide for the stop (a long
    title before a right stop), the Tab takes no width, the text
    carries on after the title and wraps like any other text.
  - A Tab with no stop before the right indent goes to the right
    indent; text after it wraps normally. A stop beyond the right
    indent is kept in the file, and a Tab that would pass the right
    indent goes to the right indent.
  - A justified line with a Tab in it is not spread out.
  - The number or bullet of a list item and its text use the same
    rules: the Tab after a label goes to the next stop, so a number
    wider than its space pushes the text to the next stop, and the
    ruler's stops count for lists too.

Borders and shading
-------------------

Word puts lines round paragraphs (borders) and a colour behind them
or behind some text (shading). !Letters draws both, and Format >
Borders and shading... changes them. It opens a box:

  Setting      None (no lines), Box (all four sides) or Custom (the
               sides ticked below)
  Borders      Top, Bottom, Left, Right and Between, to tick. Between
               is the line between two paragraphs of one box (below).
               Clicking a side makes Setting what the sides now are
  Style        Single, Double, Dotted, Dashed or Thick
  Width        1/4, 1/2, 3/4, 1, 1 1/2, 2 1/4 or 3 points
  Colour       click for the colours; Automatic is black
  Fill         None, or Colour: the paragraph's shading. Colour
               starts as the light grey Word offers; click the
               colour button for another (Automatic is not offered)
  Apply to     Paragraph (the lines and the fill go to every
               paragraph the selection touches), or Text (only the
               fill, as shading behind the selected characters, or
               behind what you type next if nothing is selected; the
               border fields are shaded)

The box shows the paragraph with the caret. If you selected several
paragraphs that differ, that field is empty and is left as it is
unless you change it. Style, Width and Colour go to the sides ticked;
in a selection whose paragraphs differ you must tick a side before
you change them, or the bell sounds. A side you tick for the first
time gets Word's gap between the line and the text: 1 point above and
below, 4 points at the left and right; a side that was there keeps
its own gap. OK (or RETURN) applies what you changed as one undo
step, and does nothing if you changed nothing; Cancel, ESCAPE or the
close icon change nothing. There is one box for each document.

The bell sounds and the box stays open, with nothing changed, when:

  - the paragraph's borders or shading come from the file in a form
    !Letters can only keep, not change: a colour taken from a theme, or
    settings that a newer Word wrote. Change those in Word. They are
    still drawn, in the colour written beside the theme's name. (A
    border or shading that comes from the paragraph's style can be
    changed: yours goes on top of the style's, and turning off a
    side the style draws writes "no line" for it.) The same for
    shading behind text whose own shading is of that kind;
  - a field holds something the box does not offer;
  - the selection changed while the box was open (the box is then
    filled again: look and press OK once more).

How it is drawn. The left and right lines are in the margin, outside
the text, and the top and bottom lines add their gap above and below
the paragraph, so the text itself does not move. Paragraphs next to
each other with the same borders (every side the same style, width,
colour and gap) and the same left and right indents are one box: the
top line is above the first, the bottom line below the last, the
Between line (if there is one) between them, and the sides run on
unbroken. If they differ in anything, or have a page break, a section
break, a table or a picture between them, they are boxes of their
own. Shading fills the box between its lines and through the space
between the paragraphs of the box, but not the space before the first
paragraph or after the last. A paragraph with a fill but no lines is
joined to the next only if it has the same colour. Shading behind
text is drawn behind the letters the whole height of the line, like a
highlight, and a highlight on the same text is drawn over it.

Lines are single, double (two lines with a gap), dotted, dashed and
thick (one solid line, as wide as its width); the other styles a
Word document can have (wave, triple, 3-D...) are drawn as single
lines and are kept as they are in the file. Widths up to 12 points
are drawn (the box offers up to 3). Shadows, frames
and the bar line are kept but not drawn. Patterns (a 25 percent
shading, say) are shown half and half and kept; the box offers only
a plain colour. Borders round text, as against shading behind it, are
kept but cannot be made. There is no key and no toolbar button.

In a table, Apply to also offers Cell and Table, for the lines and
shading of cells and tables: they are described in section 10.

What is kept
------------

Only what you change is written. The spacing a document had that
!Letters does not understand (the automatic spacing of web pages, say)
is kept as it was, and is replaced only when you set the same
thing: setting a space before, say, takes off the 'automatic'
before. Undo puts back exactly what the paragraph had, so a file
opened, changed and undone all the way saves identically.

These things are guessed, and still to be checked in real Word: the
amount of Add space after, what CTRL-0 does to a mixed selection,
the 14 points of automatic spacing, the default of Widow/orphan
control, where the baseline sits in an 'exactly' line, and the
1.08 lines of a new document's Normal style.

For tab stops, these are guesses to be checked in real Word: what a
right, centre or decimal tab does with text too wide for its stop,
how a line with a Tab and no stop before the right indent wraps, how
the leaders look, what the Tabs box does with a selection of
paragraphs with different stops, and that the Paragraph box stays
open when you press its Tabs... button (Word applies it and closes
it first).

For borders and shading, these are guesses to be checked in real
Word: that paragraphs with the same borders but another left or right
indent are separate boxes; that paragraphs with a fill and no lines
are joined only when the colour is the same; that the space before
the first paragraph of a box and after the last is not shaded; that
shading behind text has a highlight over it; how the thicker widths
and the dotted and dashed lines look; whether Apply to Text with only
a caret should shade the word the caret is in (here it shades what you
type next); and that the box beeps for a theme-coloured border that
Word itself would change.


7) Lists
--------

Bulleted and numbered lists are shown as Word shows them: the bullet
or number sits to the left of the item, in the space the list's
indents leave for it, and the item's text (and every line it wraps
onto) starts at the list's indent. You can make a list of your own
(see "Making a list" below) as well as edit the ones a document
has. !Letters shows:

  Bullets        the round bullet, the white bullet (Word's small
                 "o"), square, arrow, tick and diamond of Word's
                 lists. Word draws them with its Symbol, Courier
                 New and Wingdings fonts, which are not on this
                 disc, so !Letters draws the nearest shapes there are.
                 The round bullet, white bullet and square come
                 from the text's own font (Calibri's and Arial's
                 stand-ins have them; Cambria's stand-in has only
                 the round one); the arrow, tick and diamond are in
                 none of the disc's fonts, so they come from
                 whatever font the browser finds and may look a
                 little different from Word's
  Numbers        1. 2. 3., a) b) c), i. ii. iii., A. B., I. II.,
                 01 02, 1st 2nd, with any text the list puts
                 around them ("Article 1:", "(a)")
  Levels         lists of up to nine levels (1. then a. then i.),
                 each level further in; a level counts from the
                 start again after an item of a higher level
  Legal          numbers such as 1.1 and 1.1.1, made of the
                 numbers of the levels above
  Headings       headings numbered by their style (1, 1.1, 1.1.1),
                 as many reports are

The numbers are worked out from the whole document, so they change
as you edit: add or delete an item and the ones after it are
numbered again at once. The bullet or number is not part of the
text: the caret cannot go into it and it is never selected (a click
on it puts the caret at the start of the item's text).

Items of a list inside a table's cells count too, as in Word: the
numbers go through the table in reading order (its rows top to
bottom, each row's cells left to right). So when a list carries on
after a table whose cells hold items of the same list, the first
item after the table may not follow the one before it: after 2.,
a table holding two items of the list, the next item is 5. The
table's cells show their numbers like any list.


Making a list
-------------

Select the paragraphs (or put the caret in one) and click a button
in the second row of the toolbar, to the right of the Line spacing
button:

  Bullets       a bulleted list; the round bullet. Click it again
                (it is pressed in while all the paragraphs are
                bulleted) to take the bullets away
  Numbering     a numbered list: 1. 2. 3. Click it again to take
                the numbers away
  arrows        the small arrow after each button opens its
                gallery: None and the six bullets, or None and the
                six numberings, shown with a sample. Click one to
                make the paragraphs a list like that; the one the
                selection has is ticked, and clicking a ticked one
                takes the list away, as None does

The same keys and menu: CTRL-SHIFT-L makes the selection a bulleted
list, or takes the bullets away (as in Word; on a Mac it is CTRL, not
Cmd), and Format > List > Bullets > and Numbering > have the same two
galleries.

The bullets of the gallery are the round bullet, a white bullet, a
square, a diamond, an arrow and a tick; the numberings are 1. 2. 3.,
1) 2) 3), I. II. III., A. B. C., a) b) c) and i. ii. iii. Each goes
on in the next levels with Word's own pattern: the numbering 1.
becomes a. and then i. in the levels below it.

What happens to the paragraphs:

  - Ordinary paragraphs become items at the first level, and get
    Word's List Paragraph style (its look is a half-inch indent);
    taking them out of the list gives them their old style back.
    A paragraph in some other style (a quotation, say) keeps its
    style. Items already in a list keep their level.
  - Choosing numbers for paragraphs that are bullets (or bullets for
    numbered ones) changes the kind of the list. A list is of the
    kind of its first level, so a bulleted level inside a numbered
    list counts as numbered.
  - A paragraph made a list straight after an earlier list (with
    only ordinary paragraphs between) of the same kind and format
    goes on that list: the numbers carry on, however many ordinary
    paragraphs come between. A list of another format in between
    does not count: that starts a new list, numbered from 1.
  - A heading numbered by its style keeps its style: Numbering takes
    its number away (it counts as numbered already), and Bullets
    gives it a bullet in place of the number.
  - It is one undo step, and CTRL-Z puts everything back.


Restart, continue and set the value
-----------------------------------

Format > List has three more items for the item with the caret (or
the first selected one):

  Restart at 1              this item starts the list again at 1,
                            and so do all the items after it in the
                            list; the ones before keep their numbers
  Continue numbering        the list goes on from the list before:
                            the item and the rest of its run (the
                            items of its list around it) carry on
                            the count
  Set numbering value...    a box with two choices: Start new list,
                            with a Value (a whole number from 0 to
                            32767; 1 when it opens), and Continue
                            from previous list. OK does it; Cancel
                            or ESCAPE changes nothing. If the Value
                            is not a whole number from 0 to 32767
                            the box stays open and you hear a beep

Starting at 5 gives 5. 6. 7.; in a roman list V. VI. VII. Each is
shaded outside lists, and is one undo step.

AutoFormat as you type
----------------------

Type a marker at the very start of a paragraph that is not in a
list, then a space (or TAB): the marker and the space go, and the
paragraph is an item of a list, as in Word. The markers:

  *  -  >            a bulleted list: the round bullet, a dash,
                     an arrow
  1.  1)  (1)        a numbered list, any number from 0 to 32767
                     (no leading zero)
  a.  a)  A.         lettered: a. b. c..., a) b), A. B. (A) is not
                     a marker)
  i.  ii.  I.  IV.   Roman numbers up to 39, with the full stop

The number or letter you type must be the one that comes next if
there is a list of that kind before: typing 3. after an item 2. goes
on that list; typing any other number starts a new list at that
number (typing 7. alone makes a list that starts at 7). A single
letter that could be a Roman number (i v x l c d m) is read as
whichever goes on the list before; with no list before, i. and I.
are Roman one and the others letters (and i) is always the letter
i).

If it was not what you wanted, CTRL-Z at once gives you back the
marker and the space as you typed them; CTRL-Z again undoes the
typing. Typing on after that does not turn it into a list again.

It happens only when you type the space or TAB at the start of the
paragraph. It never happens when you paste, when an input method
(for typing other scripts) gives you text, with Find and Replace, or
with undo and redo; and not when something other than a marker comes
before the caret, a link or a field for example. A paragraph that is
in a list, or whose style is numbered, is left alone.

To switch it off, untick Format > AutoFormat lists (the last item
of the Format menu; it is ticked while AutoFormat is on). The choice
is kept for the next time, for all your documents.


Editing a list
--------------

  Enter          at the end of an item: a new item, numbered next;
                 in an empty item: the list ends there
  Tab            at the start of an item, or with items selected:
                 one level deeper; anywhere else, a tab
  SHIFT-Tab      anywhere in an item: one level higher
  Backspace      at the start of an item: its number or bullet
                 goes, the paragraph stays, and its text usually
                 stays where it was; a second Backspace joins it to
                 the paragraph before, as always

Tab goes to the next level the list has: in a list that only has
levels 1 and 3, Tab on level 1 goes to level 3. On the deepest level
Tab does nothing (it does not type a tab either), and SHIFT-Tab does
nothing on the top level. With a selection over plain paragraphs and
list items, Tab and SHIFT-Tab change only the list items. Each
change is one undo step, and undo brings the numbers back.

Format > List does the same for the selected items:

  Bullets >, Numbering >     the galleries (see "Making a list")
  Restart at 1, Continue numbering, Set numbering value...
  Demote             one level deeper (Tab)
  Promote            one level higher (SHIFT-Tab)
  Remove from list   the number or bullet goes, and the paragraph
                     goes back to its style's indents

Bullets and Numbering are shaded only when the document has no caret
and no selection at all (when it has no text to put the caret in);
the other items when no selected paragraph is in a list.

The ruler shows a list item's indents where they are drawn.
Dragging its markers, CTRL-M and CTRL-SHIFT-M, and the toolbar's
indent buttons move the item's text and its number together; the
indent you set is then the item's own, and stays when its level
changes.

Not possible yet:

  - making your own bullets or numbering (a picture bullet, another
    font, another format) or changing a list's indents as a whole;
    you choose from the galleries
  - keeping a list item's bullet or number when it comes from
    another program (it becomes an ordinary paragraph). Within one
    document a copied list item stays in its list, and pasted into
    another !Letters document it brings its list along, as a new list
    there (section 4)

Where Word may differ. These are guesses, still to be checked in
real Word:

  - Tab or SHIFT-Tab on a heading numbered by its style changes its
    level but keeps its style; Word is thought to change the style
    too (Heading 2 to Heading 3).
  - After Backspace at the start of an item, the text stays where it
    was when the number fitted in the space before the text; with a
    wider number, the first line moves to the item's indent.
  - Remove from list puts the paragraph back to its style's indents.
  - Two lists that share one list definition carry on each other's
    count, unless one of them is set to start again.
  - Letters after z go on aa, bb, cc...; a number too wide for its
    space pushes the text on to the next tab stop, as a tab in the
    text would go (every half inch from the margin unless the
    paragraph sets its own); a number aligned right stays inside its
    space.
  - The shapes of the bullets that Word draws from Symbol and
    Wingdings.
  - A new list goes on the list before even when ordinary paragraphs
    come between; Word may start a new one there.
  - Restart at 1 and Set numbering value: how Word records them (a
    new list, or a start on the item) and what it shows at the
    other levels of the list; Continue numbering on an item of a
    restarted list carries the whole run of that list on.
  - The bullets and the numbers that AutoFormat makes from *, - and
    >, and from 3. at the start of a list; and which markers Word
    changes (here: those listed above and no others).
  - List items pasted from another document: here they make a new
    list, numbered from its own start and never joined to a list
    of this document; Word may join the list it is pasted next to,
    or carry on the numbers the items had. Only whole items (their
    paragraph end selected too) keep their bullets or numbers, as
    is thought to be Word's rule.


8) Inserting: breaks, symbols, links and bookmarks
--------------------------------------------------

The Insert item of the window's menu (click MENU over the document;
Insert comes after Edit) puts something into the document at the
caret. It can put in the three kinds of break a Word document has,
a symbol, a special character (a dash, a quote, a no-break space...) or
a hyperlink, or give the selected text a name (a bookmark):

  Insert >
    Page break          CTRL-Enter
    Column break        CTRL-SHIFT-Enter (outside a table)
    Section break >     Next page, Continuous, Even page, Odd page
    Table...            make a table (section 10)
    Tab                 a tab character in a table's cell
                        (CTRL-Tab; section 10)
    Picture...          put in a PNG, JPEG or GIF picture file
                        (section 9)
    Symbol...           a window of characters (below)
    Special character > Em dash, En dash, Non-breaking space
                        (CTRL-Shift-Space), Non-breaking hyphen
                        (CTRL-Shift--), Optional hyphen, Copyright,
                        Registered, Trademark, Section, Paragraph,
                        Ellipsis, quotes, Degree
    Hyperlink...        CTRL-K: a link to a web, e-mail or ftp
                        address, or to a bookmark (below)
    Bookmark...         CTRL-Shift-F5: name the selected text
                        or the caret's place; go to or delete
                        a bookmark by its name
    Page number         the number of the page it is on
    Number of pages     how many pages the document has
    Date, Time          today's date (14/10/2026), the time (14:05)
    Field...            any field, chosen from a box (below)

Point at an item with Help running to read what it does. The items are
shaded when the document has no caret. Each one is a single step that
CTRL-Z undoes.

Page number, Number of pages, Date and Time put in a field: a piece
of text !Letters (and Word) works out for itself. Put a page number
in a footer (double-click the bottom of a page first: section 12)
and every page shows its own number. A date or time shows the date
or time the window was opened, until you put in a field or update
one there: Insert > Date or Time, Edit > Update field, Update all
fields and the Field box's OK put the date or time now into the
document, and the window shows that from then on (section 2).
Field... opens a box
with a list of the fields !Letters can work out (PAGE, NUMPAGES,
DATE, TIME, FILENAME, AUTHOR...), a Format list (1, 2, 3 or i, ii,
iii; a date as 14 October 2026...) and the field code they make,
such as PAGE \* roman \* MERGEFORMAT. You can change the code or
type any other: Word works out the ones !Letters cannot, which are
put in empty. Click OK (or press Return). With a whole field
selected (drag over it; just before a page number or a date,
SHIFT-Right selects it), the box changes that field's code instead;
a field whose code holds another field cannot be changed there.
Anywhere else, even with the caret in the text of a field !Letters
does not work out, OK puts a new field in. An
empty code beeps, says so, and keeps the box open.

Picture... asks for the path of a picture file (a PNG, JPEG or GIF
on the disc): type it, or drop the file's icon on the box, then press
Return or click Insert. The picture goes in at the caret, at its
natural size (made to fit between the margins if it is wider), and
replaces any selected text. A path that does not exist, a directory,
an address (http://...), a file that is not a PNG, JPEG or GIF
picture, or one over 20 MB is refused with a message and nothing is
changed. Nothing is ever fetched from the Internet.

You can also drop a picture file's icon on the document's window: it
goes in where you drop it, as one step CTRL-Z undoes. Of several
files dropped together the first picture goes in and any other
picture is ignored; files that are not pictures open as documents,
as before. A picture file that is not really one gets the same
message as above. Section 9 has the rest about pictures.

Symbols and special characters are described after the breaks
below, and hyperlinks and bookmarks after them.

In Print layout a page break starts a new page, and a section break
starts its section as it says (section 11). In Draft, or with View
> Formatting marks on, a break is also shown as a dotted line.
Either way it is saved in the file exactly as Word saves it, so
that Word starts the page (or the section) there when it opens the
document.

Page breaks
-----------

Press CTRL-Enter, or choose Insert > Page break, to put a page
break at the caret. (On a Mac too the key is CTRL-Enter: Cmd-Enter
is Enter.) Anything selected is deleted first. The paragraph is then
split just after the break, as Enter splits it, so the break ends
its paragraph and the text after it starts the next one; the caret
goes to the start of that paragraph. In a numbered list the next item
follows with the same number style, and in a heading the new
paragraph has the heading's style, as with Enter. This is how Word
2013 and later puts in a page break.

In a table's cell CTRL-Enter and Insert > Page break are refused
(a beep, and the menu item is shaded), and so are Column break and
Section break. In a header or footer (section 12) all three are
refused too.

In Print layout the text after a page break starts the next page.
In Draft (or with Formatting marks on) the break is shown as a grey
dotted line, labelled 'Page break', across the rest of the line it
ends (at least about an inch: if less room is left, the line is
begun on the next line). A column break (below) looks the same but
is labelled 'Column break'. Other breaks Word has (a plain new line)
are shown as before.

The break is a single character that you cannot see but can move
over, select and delete:

  Left, Right             pass the break in one press
  Shift with them         select it (the first few dots of the line
                          light up: the line itself is only a
                          picture)
  Backspace               just after the break, takes it out; at the
                          start of the paragraph after the break, it
                          first joins that paragraph to the one with
                          the break, and a second Backspace takes the
                          break out
  Delete                  just before the break, takes it out
  click on the line       puts the caret before the break if you
                          click its left half, after it if its right
  copy and paste          take the break with the text, also into
                          another !Letters document

The dotted line is only on the screen: it is not text, and Find does
not see it. A program you paste into gets a new line where the break
was.

Column breaks
-------------

Press CTRL-SHIFT-Enter outside a table, or choose Insert > Column
break, to put a column break at the caret. It goes in just as a page
break does: anything selected is deleted first, the paragraph is
split just after the break and the caret goes to the start of the
new paragraph. One step, which CTRL-Z undoes. (On a Mac too the key
is CTRL-SHIFT-Enter.)

In Print layout the text after a column break starts at the top of
the next column of a section set in columns (section 13); after the
last column of a page it starts the next page, and in a section of
one column it starts the next page, as in Word. In Draft it is shown
as a dotted line labelled 'Column break', and you move over, select
and delete it as a page break.

In a table's cell CTRL-SHIFT-Enter splits the table instead, as in
Word (section 10), and Insert > Column break is shaded.

Section breaks
--------------

A Word document is made of sections. Each can have its own page
size, orientation, margins, columns, headers and footers, and a
number at which its page numbers start. A section break ends one
section and begins the next. Choose Insert > Section break, then:

  Next page      the new section starts on a new page
  Continuous     the new section starts on the same page
  Even page      the new section starts on the next page with an
                 even number
  Odd page       the new section starts on the next page with an
                 odd number

(There are no keys for them.) Anything selected is deleted first.
Where the break goes depends on where the caret is:

  - in the middle or at the end of a paragraph, the paragraph is
    split there and the first part ends the section; the caret is
    at the start of the new section
  - at the start of a paragraph that has text, an empty paragraph
    before it ends the section, as in Word
  - in an empty paragraph, that paragraph ends the section (if it
    was the last one of its section, an empty paragraph is added
    after it for the new section to begin with)

The section before the break is a copy of the section it was cut
from: the page size and orientation, the margins, the columns, the
headers and footers, where the page numbers start (everything that
section had). The section after the break keeps the original
settings and is given the type you chose. A section break you
insert gives two sections that look the same: change either one's
paper, margins or orientation with Format > Page setup..., and how
it starts with Format > Layout... (section 11). A section with its
own page size, margins or columns is shown at its own width
(section 2). An Even page or Odd page break may need a blank page
before the new section: section 11 says how it is counted.

A section break is shown as a band under the last paragraph of the
section: a double dotted line, labelled with how the NEXT section
starts: 'Section break (Next page)', '(Continuous)', '(Even page)'
or '(Odd page)'. The last section has no band. In Print layout the
band is shown only with View > Formatting marks on. The band is
only on the screen: you cannot select it or put the caret in it; a
click in it puts the caret at the end of the paragraph above.

To take a break out again:

  Delete                  at the end of the last paragraph of a
                          section
  Backspace               at the start of the first paragraph of
                          the section after the break
  (CTRL-Delete and CTRL-Backspace do the same there)

The two sections become one. It has the page settings of the later
of the two, and the type (Next page, Continuous...) of the earlier
one. The caret stays where it is, and CTRL-Z puts the break back. A
second press then joins the paragraphs, as always. At the start of a
numbered item that begins a section, Backspace removes the break
first, and the number stays. A section that ends with a table cannot
be joined this way. Deleting a range of text across a break leaves
the sections as they are (see section 3).

Page break before
-----------------

A paragraph whose Page break before option is on (Format >
Paragraph..., in Line and page breaks: section 6; it can come from
the paragraph's style as well) starts a new page in Print layout,
as in Word, unless it is already the first thing on its page (no
empty page is made, and the document's first paragraph starts no
new page). In Draft, or with Formatting marks on, it is marked by a
small gap above it with a grey dotted line labelled 'Page break
before' (on the document's first paragraph too). Clicking in the
gap puts the caret at the start of the paragraph below. Turning the
option off (the Paragraph box, or CTRL-Z) takes the mark and the
new page away.

Keep with next, Keep lines together and Widow/orphan control have no
mark on the screen; in Print layout they decide where the pages
break (section 11). They are kept in the file and can be changed in
the Paragraph box.

Symbols and special characters
------------------------------

Insert > Symbol... opens a window called Symbol with a grid of
characters, 16 to a row (eight rows show; use the scroll bar, the
mouse wheel, PAGE UP and PAGE DOWN for the rest of a long set). From
the top down it has:

  Set               click it to choose the set: Latin-1, Latin
                    Extended-A, Greek, Cyrillic, Punctuation,
                    Currency, Letterlike and fractions, Arrows,
                    Maths or Geometric shapes
  Recently used     the last 16 characters you put in, newest
                    first, kept until you quit
  the grid          click a character to choose it, double-click it
                    to put it in
  a line of text    says which character is chosen, as U+00E9
                    Latin-1 (or why one could not go in)
  Insert, Close     Insert puts the chosen character in; Close
                    shuts the window

The keys work while the window has the caret: the arrow keys move
the choice, HOME and END go to the ends of the set, RETURN inserts
and ESCAPE closes the window.

The character goes in at the caret of the document you last clicked
in (a selection is replaced), so click in the text, choose, and
click Insert; the window stays open, so click in the text again for
the next one. It goes in as ordinary text in the font of the text
there; a bold or italic you have asked for with CTRL-B or CTRL-I at
the caret is used. Each symbol is a step of its own that CTRL-Z
undoes. If no document has been clicked in, or the one you used has
been closed, the bell sounds and the window says so: click in a
document first. A symbol typed after 1. does not start a list, as a
space does (section 7).

!Letters does not use Word's Symbol font: a symbol is a character like
any other in the file, so Word shows it as it would the same
character typed. The sets hold only characters that the fonts !Letters
draws Calibri, Arial and Times New Roman with have, so the
characters look right in them; in some other fonts (Cambria has no
Greek or Cyrillic) a character may be drawn from another font.

Insert > Special character > puts in one thing, for the commonest
needs (the keys are shown on the menu):

  Em dash, En dash, Copyright, Registered, Trademark, Section,
  Paragraph, Ellipsis, Single opening quote, Single closing quote,
  Double opening quote, Double closing quote, Degree
                          the character, like a symbol
  Non-breaking space      CTRL-SHIFT-Space: a space that never
                          ends a line (typed after 1. it does not
                          start a list)
  Non-breaking hyphen     CTRL-SHIFT--: a hyphen that never ends a
                          line (it is kept in the file as Word
                          keeps it, not as a hyphen character)
  Optional hyphen         shows nothing unless a line ends at it,
                          where Word shows a hyphen (!Letters shows
                          nothing there yet); same file form

A non-breaking hyphen is a hyphen when you copy it or look for it
with Find (Find does not change it with Replace); an optional hyphen
is nothing. They take the bold, italic and the rest that the text
before them has, and a bold or italic asked for at the caret.

On a Mac, CTRL-SHIFT-Space may be used by the computer to change the
keyboard layout, and in some web browsers CTRL-SHIFT-- zooms out
before !Letters sees it: the Special character menu does the same.

Hyperlinks
----------

A hyperlink is text that stands for an address. Select the text
(or put the caret where the link should go) and press CTRL-K (Cmd-K
on a Mac), or choose Insert > Hyperlink.... The box, called Insert
hyperlink, has:

  Text to display   the selected text; change it if you like. With
                    only a caret, type the words the link is to show
  Address           where the link goes: a web address (http:// or
                    https://), an e-mail address (mailto:), a file
                    server (ftp://), or an address that starts www.,
                    to which !Letters puts http:// in front
  Bookmark          a popup of the document's bookmarks: choose one
                    and #name goes into Address, a link to that
                    place in the same document
  ScreenTip         words Word shows when the pointer rests on the
                    link (optional; !Letters does not show them)
  Remove link       shaded until you are changing a link
  Cancel, OK

Click OK (or press RETURN). The text turns blue and underlined, as
in Word; it keeps its own bold, italic and size, and the caret goes
after the link. The first time, !Letters adds Word's Hyperlink style to
the document for the blue and the underline. One link is one step
that CTRL-Z undoes, the style and the address included.

To change a link, put the caret next to it or select it and press
CTRL-K again: the box is called Edit hyperlink and shows the link's
text, address and ScreenTip. Change what you like and click OK, or
click Remove link: the words stay, as plain text with the look they
had, and only the link goes. Each is one undo step. Changing the
words of a link puts them in as one piece of text, in the look of
the link's first part. A link is one item to the arrow keys and to
Backspace and Delete: Left and Right pass it in one press and Delete
takes the whole link away.

Addresses are checked. Only http, https, mailto and ftp are accepted:
javascript:, data:, file:, vbscript: and any other kind are refused
with a beep and a message in the box, and so is an address with no
host (http://), with a character that is not allowed (a control
character) or longer than 2048 characters. A space or an accented
letter in an address is written the way a web address writes it (%20
and so on). The box holds at most 1000 characters; a longer address
that came in a file is kept as it is (its field is shaded) and you can
still change the link's words or its ScreenTip. A link whose address
the file does not give (Word may leave it out) shows an empty
address, and you can type one.

!Letters never opens a link and never asks your browser to: an address
is only text to it, and nothing is fetched. A click on a web link just
puts the caret in it; copy the link and paste it into a web page or a
mail program to use it (the paste is an ordinary link), or open the file
in Word. A link to a bookmark can be followed: hold CTRL (Cmd on a Mac)
and click it, and the caret goes to the bookmark and the window scrolls
there. If your mouse is set to the Acorn mapping (rightIsAdjust),
CTRL-click is the Menu button, and on a Mac it may be a click with the
right button; then use the Bookmark box and Go to.

Bookmarks
---------

A bookmark gives a name to a piece of text or to a place in the
document, so that you (or a link) can come back to it. Select the
text or put the caret in the place, and press CTRL-SHIFT-F5, or
choose Insert > Bookmark... (shaded when the document has no caret).
The Bookmark box has:

  Name              type the name of a new bookmark
  popup             the names the document has already: choose one
                    and it goes into Name (at most 200 are listed;
                    the box says so, and any name can be typed)
  Sort by           Name or Location
  Hidden bookmarks  tick it to list the ones whose names start with
                    an underline too (see below)
  Add, Delete, Go to, Close

Add makes the bookmark (RETURN does the same) and shuts the box;
Delete takes the named bookmark away (the box stays, and says so);
Go to selects the bookmarked text, or puts the caret at an empty
bookmark, and scrolls to it (the box stays, so you can try another);
Close shuts the box. A wrong name or a name that is not there beeps
and says why in the box. Each Add and Delete is one step that CTRL-Z
undoes; Go to is none.

Names follow Word: they begin with a letter, then have letters,
digits and underlines, with no spaces, up to 40 characters. Capitals
do not matter: Add of a name that exists (Chapter1 and chapter1)
moves that bookmark to the new place instead of making a second one.
A bookmark may be over one word or several paragraphs, may hold
another or overlap one, and an empty one marks a place. A bookmark
cannot start or end in a table's edge: select text, not the table.
A bookmark may be in a cell: Add, Go to and Delete work on it as
anywhere. Bookmarks that sit between a table's rows or cells (the
file may have some) are kept and listed, but cannot be moved or
deleted here. Go to cannot take you into a table that is a grey box.

Hidden bookmarks are those whose names start with an underline, such
as _Toc123456 (a table of contents entry) or _GoBack (where you last
edited). Word makes them for itself; !Letters lists them only when
Hidden bookmarks is ticked, keeps them, but never makes one. A name
that Word would not accept (it begins with a digit, has a space or
is over 40 characters) can come in a file: !Letters lists it, goes to
it and deletes it, but would not make it.

What deleting does. A bookmark is never torn in two: when a deletion
(Backspace or Delete on a selection, typing or pasting over it, Cut)
takes away only one end of a bookmark, that end is put back where the
text was, so the bookmark gets shorter instead of breaking (also
when that end was in a table the deletion takes whole). When the
deletion takes away the whole bookmark, it goes. Deleting one
character beside a bookmark's mark with Backspace or Delete leaves
the mark (section 3). Text typed at an empty bookmark, between its
two marks, goes inside it; text typed just after its end, or before
its start, goes outside. Copying text that holds a bookmark does not
copy the bookmark: a name is in one place only.

What is kept
------------

Everything a break had in the file stays when the break is taken out
and put back (CTRL-Z puts the document back exactly as it was, so a
document opened, changed and undone all the way saves the same
bytes). Section settings that !Letters does not understand are copied
with the rest, and the original section keeps all of its own.

These things are guessed, and still to be checked in real Word: the
paragraph left holding just the break when you press CTRL-Enter at
the start of a numbered item or a heading (it keeps its number or
heading style here); whether the section before a break should
really have the same headers and page-number start as the one after
(Word copies them); what a merged section takes from the two; that
Word also leaves the sections alone when text is deleted across a
break; that Backspace at the start of a numbered item that begins a
section should take the break first; that a break is labelled by the
type of the section after it; and the first paragraph's mark for
Page break before.

For symbols and special characters, these are guesses to be checked
in real Word: that a file with a non-breaking or optional hyphen
opens and behaves as it should; that Word shows every character of
the sets in Calibri (or what it falls back to); what a symbol does
typed over a character in overwrite mode (here it replaces it, like
typed text); and that the browser leaves CTRL-SHIFT-- alone.

For links and bookmarks, these are guesses to be checked in real
Word: that Word opens the file with its links and bookmarks without
a prompt and shows the same addresses, ScreenTips and names; that a
link's old address, left in the file when you change or remove a
link, does no harm (it is not used); that taking a link away from
text that had a character style of its own (Emphasis, say) leaves it
without that style; how Word treats a bookmark when one end is
deleted, and text typed beside a bookmark; and that Word accepts the
bookmarks whose numbers are out of the ordinary that !Letters keeps.


9) Pictures
-----------

!Letters shows the pictures in a Word document, and lets you select
them, change their size, delete them, give them alt text, put in new
ones, and copy them to another !Letters document. It works with PNG,
JPEG and GIF pictures, the three kinds a Word document most often
holds.

What is shown
-------------

A PNG, JPEG or GIF picture is drawn in the line of text where it
is, at the size the document gives it (96 pixels to the inch, at
zoom 100%), with the crop the document asks for. A picture that is
wider than the text is shrunk, in proportion, to fit between the
margins, and one taller than a line can be (2112 pixels) is shrunk
in proportion to that; the document itself keeps the size it had
until you change the picture's size. A line with a picture on it is
as tall as the picture. A picture that does not fit in what is left
of a line goes on to the next one, even straight after a word or
another picture, as in Word. A picture is one character: the caret
moves over it and Delete takes it out, as it does a letter.

A picture that floats (one Word places on the page and flows text
round: "In front of text", "Square" and so on) is placed on its
page in Print layout, where the document puts it, and the text
flows round it, over it or under it as its Wrap text says (section
13 has the details, and Format > Wrap text, which changes it). In
Draft, Web layout and Outline, and in a header or footer in every
view, it is shown in its paragraph, where it is anchored, with a
dashed outline to mark it, taking room in the line as a picture in
the text does; so is a floating picture whose place the document
gives in a way !Letters does not understand.

Every other picture or drawing is a grey box of its size with a
label, and is kept exactly as it is in the file:

  Picture not shown: EMF    a kind !Letters does not draw (EMF, WMF,
                            SVG, BMP, TIFF...; the kind is named)
  Picture missing           the file has no picture for it
  Picture too large to show an image over 20 MB or too many pixels
                            (see "Safe limits" below)
  Loading picture...        shown for a moment while many pictures
                            are being prepared

An old-style (VML) picture, a chart, a shape, a text box and the
like are boxes too: !Letters does not draw them, and does not change
them. Pictures in headers and footers are shown on every page that
shows the header, and can be put in and changed there as in the text
(section 12); pictures in footnotes are not shown yet. Pictures in
a table's cells are shown as in any paragraph, and a click on one
selects it, as anywhere (see section 10 for pictures in cells).

While a picture is being prepared its place is blank, or shows its
alt text. A picture is prepared only when it comes on the screen, so
a document with many pictures still opens quickly.

Selecting a picture
-------------------

  click on it         selects it: it is tinted and eight small
                      black squares (handles) appear at its corners
                      and the middle of its sides
  SHIFT-Left, -Right  from beside it, selects it (as for a letter)
  double-click        selects it and opens the Picture box (below)

Click elsewhere, or press Escape, to end the selection. Typing
over a selected picture replaces it, as with a selected word, and
Delete or Backspace takes it out. A picture can be cut, copied and
pasted like text; CTRL-Z puts it back. With the format painter on,
a click on a picture does what it does on text.

Changing a picture's size
-------------------------

Press on a handle of the selected picture and drag. A dashed
outline shows the size it will have; let go and the picture changes
to that, as one step that CTRL-Z takes back.

  corner handle           keeps the proportions: the corner follows
                          the pointer along the side that is
                          changed the more, in proportion
  SHIFT, while dragging   frees a corner, so both sides follow
                          the pointer
  side handle             changes that side only
  Escape, while dragging  cancels: nothing changes

A picture can be from 0.01 to 22 inches a side. Dragged past
those, the picture stops at the limit and a corner keeps its
proportions as far as it can; if it cannot, the bell sounds. A
picture shrunk to fit the margins changes in proportion to its size
in the document, not to the size you see, so its proportions stay
right. If you drag it back to where it started, nothing changed and
there is no step. The window does not scroll while you drag.

A floating picture is resized the same way, and keeps its place. In
Print layout it can also be moved: press on the selected picture
itself (not a handle) and drag; a dashed outline follows the
pointer, and when you let go the picture is put there, as one step
that CTRL-Z takes back. It stays on the page of the paragraph it is
anchored in: dragged past the top or the foot of the page, it
stops there (sideways it is not held on the paper).
In the other views it cannot be moved. A floating picture whose
place the document gives in a way !Letters does not understand,
or cannot change, beeps when you press on it, and a picture whose
size the document does not give in the usual place cannot be
resized (it beeps); their alt text can still be changed. If the
document changes
while you drag (you press a key, say), the drag ends with a beep and
nothing is put in.

The Picture box
---------------

Double-click a picture, or select it and choose Format > Picture...
on the window's menu:

  Width, Height    the size in inches (two decimals, 0.01 to 22)
  Keep proportions on at first: changing one of Width and Height
                   changes the other in proportion when you click
                   OK (if you change both, both are used)
  Reset size       fills in the picture's natural size: its pixels
                   divided by the resolution its image file gives
                   (dots per inch or per centimetre; 96 to the inch
                   when the file gives none), so a photo made at
                   300 dots to the inch is a third the size of the
                   same pixels at 96
  Alt text         the description a screen reader says; clear it
                   to remove it. Tabs and line breaks are allowed,
                   other control characters are not; at most 1024
                   characters
  OK, Cancel       OK makes the change (one undo step); Cancel,
                   Escape or the close icon changes nothing

A size outside 0.01 to 22 inches, a Width or Height left empty, or
text that is not a length is refused: the bell sounds, the box says
why and stays open; nothing is rounded to fit. Reset size puts its
size in the fields but does not apply it until you click OK. For a
floating picture the size fields, Keep proportions and Reset size
are shaded: only the alt text can be changed (drag a handle to
resize it, above; Reset size is also
shaded when the picture's file gives no size). Alt text that is over
1024 characters in the file is shaded and left as it is.

Insert > Picture...
-------------------

The Insert menu's Picture... (see section 8) opens a box with a
Path field. Type the pathname of a PNG, JPEG or GIF picture on the
disc, or drop the picture file's icon on the box to fill it in,
then press Return or click Insert. The picture goes in at the caret,
replacing any selected text, at its natural size (as Reset size
works it out; made smaller, in proportion, if it is wider than the
text, taller than 9 inches, or wider than 6.5 inches; the width of
the text is that of the caret's section, or one of its columns of
text, less any gutter at its side), and it is selected. It is one
step that CTRL-Z takes back, with the picture's file.

The box refuses with a message, and stays, a path that is empty,
that does not exist or is a directory, an address (anything with
"://" in it: !Letters never fetches a picture from the web), a file
that is not a PNG, JPEG or GIF picture (it looks inside the file, not
at its name), one over 20 MB, and one with more than 16384 pixels a
side or more than 64 million in all (and, in a document with no
free name or number left for a new picture, says so). Cancel or
Escape stops it, even
while a big file is being read; if the document changes while the
file is read, nothing is put in and a message says so.

Dropping a file
---------------

Drop a PNG, JPEG or GIF file's icon on a document's window and the
picture goes in where you drop it, as one step; the picture is
selected. Files dropped together: the first picture goes in; any
other picture is ignored; a file that is not a picture opens as a
document, as before (or says why it cannot). The limits above apply.
Dropped while an input method is composing, it is refused with a
beep.

Pasting a picture
-----------------

CTRL-V pastes a picture when the clipboard holds a PNG, JPEG or GIF
file and no text (an image copied in a browser or a paint program,
say, or a picture file copied in a file manager): it goes in at the
caret, in place of any selection, as Insert > Picture... does. When
the clipboard has text as well (a spreadsheet's cells, with a
picture of them), the text is pasted, as before. Of several files,
the first picture is used. A picture is not read if it is over
20 MB.

Pictures between documents
--------------------------

Copy a picture (alone, or with text round it) and paste it in the
same document, and it comes with the same picture file, a copy with
numbers of its own. Pasted into another !Letters document, it comes
with a copy of its picture file, at the same size, with its crop
and alt text; CTRL-Z takes both away. These are not carried to
another document, and are left out of the paste: a picture that
carries a hyperlink, an SVG version or an artistic effect, a
picture whose file is kept outside the document (linked), a
picture over 20 MB, and the pictures of one paste beyond the first
1000 or the first 200 MB. Old-style (VML) drawings, charts and
shapes are left out across documents too. A crop copied between a
Strict and an ordinary (Transitional) Word document is rewritten in
the other's form; one !Letters cannot read is removed, so such a
picture comes uncropped. If a pasted picture cannot get a free name
or id (there is no free number for its file, say) the paste comes
as plain text, without its pictures; when there is no text, as when
a picture alone was copied, nothing is pasted, the bell sounds and
a message says "This picture could not be pasted: no free name or
id."

A table that holds a picture comes whole, picture and all, when you
copy and paste it, within one document or into another (section
10): the picture comes by the rules above (one with a hyperlink or
an SVG version is left out in another document). A table !Letters
keeps exactly as it was (section 16) that holds a picture comes as
text, without the picture.

Pictures in a web page, mail or Word document that you paste into
!Letters do not come (see section 4): only a picture file on the
clipboard by itself does.

What is kept
------------

All of a picture's file is kept in what you save. A picture or
drawing !Letters does not understand, or that you have not changed,
is written back exactly as it was; so is a picture whose size
or alt text you changed, in everything but that. Deleting a picture
leaves its picture file in the document, unused (Word is expected
to drop it the next time it saves: still to be checked in real
Word).

Safe limits
-----------

A picture file is never run or fetched: only its pixels are
drawn. A picture file over 20 MB, wider or taller than 16384 pixels,
or of over 64 million pixels in all is not drawn (it is a labelled
box); Insert > Picture..., a drop and a paste refuse such a file
with a message. A damaged picture file is a box too. Nothing is
ever loaded from the web: a picture that a document links to on a
site is a box.

Not possible yet
----------------

  - text on both sides of a floating picture: the text takes the
    wider side only (section 13)
  - floating pictures in headers and footers placed on the page:
    they are shown in the header's text, with a dashed outline
  - Through (a Wrap text kind Word has): a picture that has it is
    shown as Tight, and the Wrap text menu ticks nothing for it
  - turning a picture, cropping it with the mouse or changing the
    crop, borders, shadows and other effects: they are kept and
    shown as far as !Letters draws them (the crop is), but cannot be
    changed
  - other kinds of picture (EMF, WMF, SVG, BMP, TIFF, charts,
    shapes, text boxes) shown, or put in
  - pictures in footnotes: they are kept, and are not shown
  - a photo's EXIF "turn" is not applied to the size !Letters works
    out: a phone photo taken upright may come in the wrong shape,
    so use the Picture box to put it right
  - 1.5 or double line spacing on a line with a picture: the line
    is as tall as the picture and the text's descent, and !Letters
    does not leave the gap Word may
  - a picture raised or lowered on its line (Word's position
    setting): its bottom is always on the baseline
  - a picture that is a link (inside a hyperlink, as a linked logo
    often is): it is shown as a grey box and kept exactly as it is;
    Insert > Hyperlink on a selection that holds a picture is
    refused ("A picture cannot be made a link yet.")

Still to be checked in real Word: that it opens the files !Letters
saves, with pictures put in, resized, deleted or pasted, without a
repair message; that its own size reset gives the sizes Reset size
does; and what it does with a picture that shares its run of text
with a word.


10) Tables
-----------

!Letters shows the tables in a Word document: their lines, their
shading, their widths, their merged cells and the look their table
style gives them. You can put the caret in their cells, move about
in them, select cells, rows and columns, type, delete, format and
find in them. You can make a table (Insert > Table...), add and
delete rows and columns, merge and split cells, split a table in
two and make rows or columns even: they are on the Table menu
(below). You can set a table's width, alignment and indent, its
rows' heights and the width and vertical alignment of columns and
cells (Table properties...), give cells and tables lines and
shading (Format > Borders and shading...), and drag the edges of
columns and rows on the page. You can copy cells, rows and whole
tables and paste them as tables (within a document, into another,
into a cell or over cells), copy tables to and from other
programs, and convert text to a table and a table to text
(Table > Convert).

What is shown
-------------

  Lines       the borders of the table and of its cells, in the
              style (single, double, dashed...), width and colour
              the document gives, with the diagonal lines some
              cells have. Where two cells meet one line is drawn,
              the heavier of the two.
  Shading     the fill of the table and of each cell
  Widths      the columns as the file gives them; a table may be
              wider than the page's text, even than the page:
              the window then scrolls sideways as far as its
              right edge, and the part beyond the page is drawn
              on the grey desk
  Merged      cells joined across columns or down rows, drawn as
  cells       one cell
  Rows        as tall as their text needs; a row that asks for a
              least height is at least that high, and one that
              asks for an exact height is that high, with the
              text cut off at the cell's edge
  Cell text   laid out in the cell's width, with the cell's
              margins, in its own fonts, sizes and colours, and
              at the top, middle or bottom of the cell as the
              cell asks; with its list numbers and bullets, and
              its pictures
  Spacing     the gaps between cells, when the table has them
  Nested      a table inside a cell is drawn the same way, down
  tables      to eight tables deep; a table deeper still is a
              grey box
  Table       Table Grid, the Grid Table and List Table styles
  styles      and others in the file: the first row (the header),
              the first and last column, the last row, and the
              bands of rows and columns, as the table's own
              options switch them on (Word's Table Design tab)

The colours of table styles are often "theme" colours, which
change with the document's theme in Word. !Letters shows them by the
ordinary colour the style gives beside the theme colour, and does
not apply the theme's tints or shades, so a style's colour may
differ a little from Word's. The file keeps the theme colour.

A big table is no slower to scroll than a long page: only the rows
in the window are drawn.

Kept, but not shown
-------------------

Some things in a table are kept in the file and saved, but have no
effect on what you see:

  - a floating table (one that text flows round) in Draft, Web
    layout and Outline: it is drawn in the text at its place, with
    a dashed outline. In Print layout it is placed on its page and
    the text flows round it (section 13)
  - text that runs up or down in a cell, and tables that run right
    to left: the cells are drawn left to right, the text across

Header rows that repeat on each page, rows that may not break
across pages and rows split between pages are shown in Print
layout: see "Tables across pages" in section 11.

A grey box instead
------------------

A few tables are not understood well enough to be drawn. Each is a
grey box labelled "[table - preserved, not shown]", is saved exactly
as it was, and can be selected, copied and deleted as a whole. They
are tables with tracked changes in the table itself (a row inserted,
a width changed with Track changes on), with content controls
wrapped round rows or cells, with some old ways of joining cells,
with a cell whose last thing is another table (Word needs a
paragraph after it, and adding one would change the file), or with
something else unusual in how the table is built. Tracked changes in
the text inside cells do not make this happen: the text is shown as
in any paragraph.

Working in a table's cells
--------------------------

The caret goes into the cells like any text. Click in a cell to put
it there. Left and Right pass from the end of a cell's text to the
start of the next cell's, row by row; Up and Down go to the line
above or below in the same place across, through the cells of a row
above or below and out of the table. A cell that is joined down
several rows is one cell: the caret is in its top part, and no key
or click puts it in the hidden lower parts (Left and Right go
straight past them, as Up and Down do). Home and End go to the
start and end of the line, in the cell.

  Tab           the next cell: its text is selected, so typing
                replaces it. In the last cell Tab adds a new row
                at the end of the table, like the last one, and
                puts the caret in its first cell (one undo step:
                CTRL-Z takes the row away and puts the caret back
                in the last cell). A cell merged down to the last
                row is not made longer: the new row has a cell of
                its own there, so you can type in every cell
  Shift-Tab     the previous cell, the same way; nothing at the
                first cell
  CTRL-Tab      a tab character in the cell. Some browsers keep
                CTRL-Tab to change their own tabs and never pass it
                on, so it may not work in yours; the Tab item of
                the Table menu and of the Insert menu does the
                same (shaded unless the caret, or text selected,
                is in one cell: not with cells selected)
  Enter         a new paragraph in the cell; Enter at the very
                start of the first cell of a table that has
                nothing before it puts an empty paragraph before
                the table, as in Word
  CTRL-SHIFT-Enter  splits the table, as in Word: the rows from the
                caret's row on become a second table, with an
                empty paragraph between the two (the caret goes
                there). In the first row it puts an empty
                paragraph before the table. Outside a table it
                puts in a column break (section 8). One undo step.
                On a Mac too it is CTRL,
                not COMMAND (COMMAND-SHIFT-Enter makes a line
                break)
  Tab in a list in a cell moves to the next cell, not down a level.

Selecting cells
---------------

  - Drag from one cell into another: the cells in the rectangle
    between them are selected and shown shaded, each in its own
    box. A cell joined with others widens the rectangle to hold
    it whole.
  - Shift-click, or Shift with the arrow keys, from one cell into
    another does the same.
  - Click just inside a cell's left edge (a few pixels): the cell's
    text is selected. Click just to the left of a row (outside the
    table): the whole row is selected. Click just above a column,
    over the table's top edge: the whole column is selected.
  - Select all, or a selection from before the table to after it,
    takes the whole table, shaded as one box.
  - Text selected within one cell is text, as anywhere. A selection
    from text outside the table into it takes the text and the rows
    it reaches, whole. (Copying and pasting cells: see "Copying and
    pasting tables" below.)

Making a table
--------------

Put the caret where the table is to go and choose Insert > Table...
(or Table > Insert table...). The Insert table box asks for:

  Columns       1 to 63 (it starts at 2)
  Rows          1 to 1000 (it starts at 2)
  Column width  Auto: the columns are equal and together fill the
                width of the text there: in a cell, the cell; in a
                section set out in two or more columns of text,
                one of those columns; in a section with its own
                page size (a landscape page), that page's width;
                in a section with a gutter at the side (not at
                the top), the width left once the gutter is
                taken off.
                Fixed: each column is the width you type beside
                it, 0.1 to 22 inches, and the table keeps those
                widths

Click OK or press Return; Cancel or Escape closes the box. A number
that is not a whole number in its range, a width outside 0.1 to 22
inches (23 inches, 0.05 or -1 is refused, never rounded into the
range), or Fixed columns together wider than 22 inches beeps and
says why in the box, which stays open. So does a caret that is
already in a table eight tables deep, and a document with no caret
in it. If you moved the caret or changed the document while the
box was open, the first click on OK beeps and says so; click OK
again (or choose Insert > Table... again) to put the table at the
caret as it is now.

The table goes in as one undo step, with a line round every cell
(Word's "Table Grid" style: !Letters adds it to the document the first
time, unless the document has its own; with it "Normal Table", the
plain style tables start from, unless the document already has a
style of that kind). The caret goes in the first cell. A table in a
cell is a nested table, as wide as the cell. Where the caret is:

  - in the middle of a paragraph: the paragraph is split and the
    table goes between the halves
  - at the start of a paragraph, or in an empty one: the table goes
    before it
  - at the end of a paragraph: the table goes after it
  - with text selected: the text is deleted first

Each cell takes the paragraph look of the paragraph the caret was
in (its style, indents, even a list). A table never ends a cell or
a section and never touches another table: !Letters puts an empty
paragraph after or between where needed, as Word does.

The Table menu
--------------

Click MENU over the document: Table comes after Format. All its
items but Insert table... and Convert > Text to table... are shaded
unless the caret or the selected cells are in a table (Insert
table... is shaded only when there is no caret). Each item is one
undo step; one that cannot be
done beeps. The menu:

  Table >
    Insert table...      the box above
    Insert >             Rows above, Rows below, Columns to the
                         left, Columns to the right
    Delete >             Rows, Columns, Table
    Select >             Cell, Row, Column, Table
    Merge cells
    Split cells...       the Split cells box (below)
    Split table          CTRL-SHIFT-Enter
    Distribute rows evenly
    Distribute columns evenly
    Cell alignment >     Top, Centre, Bottom (below)
    Table properties...  the Table properties box (below)
    Convert >            Text to table..., Table to text... (below)
    Tab                  CTRL-Tab: a tab character in the cell

Point at an item with Help running to read what it does. Word's own
keys for these (ALT-chords) are not used; the keys are the ones
shown on the menu.

Adding and deleting rows and columns
------------------------------------

The rows and columns acted on are those of the selected cells, or
of the cell the caret is in (or the text selected in one cell): as
many are added or deleted as are selected. A cell merged across
several columns counts as all of them.

  - Rows above, Rows below: new rows, each like the row beside it
    (its height and cell settings; empty cells with the paragraph
    look of the cell's first paragraph). A row added inside a cell
    merged down (between two of its rows) makes that cell longer;
    a row added below its last row, or above it, gets a cell of its
    own there. A new row stays a header row only if it is among
    header rows. Tab in the last cell does the same below the last
    row.
  - Columns to the left, Columns to the right: new columns of the
    same width as the ones selected. If the table was no wider than
    the text and would now be wider, all its columns are made
    narrower so that it is as wide as before. A cell merged across
    the new column becomes wider instead of getting a new cell.
  - Delete > Rows, Columns: the cells go with what is in them. The
    caret goes to the next row (or column), or the one before if
    there is none. Deleting every row or column deletes the table:
    the caret goes after it (an empty paragraph is added if
    nothing would be left there). Delete > Table does the same for
    the whole table. A bookmark with one end in what goes and the
    other staying is not broken: its end moves to the start of the
    next row or cell, or the end of the table; when the whole table
    goes, to where the caret goes. CTRL-Z after a column command
    puts the caret in the table's first row (a long table scrolls
    to its top).
  - Select > Cell, Row, Column, Table select them, as the clicks in
    the zones do (above). Selecting is not an undo step. Merged
    cells make the selection wider than asked.

Merging and splitting cells
---------------------------

Merge cells makes the selected cells (two or more, in a rectangle)
one cell, across the columns and rows they cover. The text of the
cells goes into it in reading order, paragraph after paragraph;
cells that were empty add nothing. The merged cell takes the look
(shading, margins) of the first cell of each row. Merge cells is
shaded for one cell, and for a selection that cannot be made
whole (it has cells at the edge of a short row, or merged cells that
reach outside it); select a larger rectangle and try again. The
hidden lower parts of a cell already merged down keep their text
hidden; it is not brought into the merged cell. Bookmarks go with
the text they mark; if one would then end before it starts (its
start was in a cell beside the rectangle), its start moves to the
beginning of the merged cell, so Word keeps it. CTRL-Z after Merge
cells puts the caret back, not the selected cells.

A cell that a file marks as the lower part of a merge but that has
no merged cell above it is shown as a cell of its own (as Word shows
it). Adding or deleting rows, merging or splitting next to it never
joins it to a merge, so its text never disappears.

Split cells... opens the Split cells box: Columns and Rows (1 to
63; it starts at 2 columns and 1 row). The caret may be in the cell,
or in a merged cell (the whole merged cell is split); if several
cells are selected they are merged first and then split, as one
step, as Word does. The cell's text stays in the first new cell;
the others are empty.

  - Columns: a cell merged over that many columns or more is
    divided among them, and the other rows keep their lines. For a
    single cell, the table gets new column lines at equal spaces,
    and the cells of the other rows over them become wider.
  - Rows: a cell merged down several rows is divided into that many
    parts, so Rows must divide the number of rows it covers
    (otherwise the box says so). A single cell gets new rows below
    it; the cells beside it are joined down over them.

A number out of range, one by one, a table that would have over 63
columns or 32767 rows, and a caret outside a table, each beep and
say why in the box, which stays open. If you moved the caret or
changed the document while the box was open, the first OK beeps
and says so, keeping the numbers you typed; click OK again to split
the cell the caret is in now.

Splitting a table
-----------------

Split table (CTRL-SHIFT-Enter) cuts the table at the caret's row:
the rows from there on become a second table, with an empty
paragraph between the two, where the caret goes. In the first row
it puts an empty paragraph before the table instead. A cell merged
down across the cut starts again in the second table. The two
tables keep their style and widths. Outside a table the key puts in
a column break, as in Word (section 8), and the menu item is
shaded.

Making rows and columns even
----------------------------

Distribute columns evenly gives the selected columns the same width
(their total, shared; the last may be a few twentieths of a point
wider). Distribute rows evenly makes the selected rows at least as
tall as the tallest of them. With only the caret in the table, all
the columns or rows are done. A row may still grow for its text.

Table properties
----------------

Table > Table properties... opens a box for the table the caret or
the selected cells are in (the item is shaded outside a table; the
box beeps if it is asked for there). It shows nine things:

  Table width   Auto (as wide as the columns make it) or Length,
                with the width beside it
  Alignment     Left, Centre or Right: where the table sits
                between the margins
  Indent from   how far the table is moved in from the left; it
  left          may be negative (-22 to 22 inches)
  Row height    Auto, At least or Exactly, with the height beside
                it (0 to 22 inches); for the rows of the selection
                (or the caret's row)
  Column width  the width of the selected columns, or of the
                caret's column
  Cell width    the width of the selected cells
  Vertical      Top, Centre or Bottom: where the text sits in the
  alignment     cell

Lengths are in inches and are 0.02 to 22 inches (Row height may be 0;
Indent may be -22). A number outside that is refused: the bell sounds
and the box says which field, and stays open. It is never rounded into
the range. A field that shows nothing (the rows or cells selected
differ, or the file gives the value in a way !Letters can only keep, a
percentage say) is left as it is unless you fill it in; a length field
you empty is also left as it is, not cleared. If the rows differ in
kind, Row height shows empty: choose Auto, At least or Exactly before
you type a length. In the same way, a table whose width the file
gives as a percentage (a table 100% wide, say) opens with Table
width empty and the width field greyed out: choose Auto or Length
first (Length puts the table's width in the field). If a width
still comes with neither, the bell sounds and the box says "Choose
Auto or Length for the table width." Lengths are compared as the box
shows them, to a hundredth of an inch: a smaller change is no change,
and changing only At least to Exactly keeps the height exactly as it
was. OK (or RETURN) puts in only what you changed, as
one undo step, and does nothing if you changed nothing; Cancel or
ESCAPE closes the box. If the selection or the document changed while
the box was open, the first OK beeps and fills the box again: look,
then press OK once more.

Column width changes the grid columns and the width of every cell
over them; a cell merged across columns takes their sum. Cell
width changes only the cell's own preferred width. A table whose
width is Auto and that Word lays out from its text may be drawn
at the width of the page's text again; set Table width to Length
to keep a size. A row that is Exactly cuts its text off at the
cell's edge.

Cell alignment > Top, Centre, Bottom sets the vertical alignment
of the selected cells (or the caret's cell) at once, one undo step.
In a cell merged down, the whole cell is aligned.

Lines and shading of cells and tables
-------------------------------------

Format > Borders and shading... (section 6) works on tables too.
With the caret in a table, or cells selected, Apply to offers two
more choices, Cell and Table, and Cell is what the box opens with.
Setting gains All, and the sides offered are:

  Top, Bottom, Left, Right   the outer edges of the selected
                             cells (or of the table)
  Inside horizontal,         the lines between the selected cells
  Inside vertical            (or between the cells of the table)

Between is shaded for Cell and Table (it is for paragraphs), and
the inside sides and All are shaded for Paragraph and Text. When
you change Apply to between the two kinds the box is filled in
again. Style, Width and Colour go to the sides ticked, as in
section 6. Fill is None or a colour: for Cell it is the shading of
each selected cell, for Table the table's own shading.

  Cell    the lines and fill of the cells selected. The box shows
          what is drawn now, including the lines a table style
          gives. A side is ticked when every selected cell has it.
          A line you put on is written on the selected cells only
          (where two cells meet the heavier of their lines is
          drawn, so it shows). A line you take off the outer edge
          of the selection is also taken off the cells next to it
          across that edge, so that neither draws it; a cell next
          to it that is wider than the selection (or reaches past
          its edge) is left alone, so its own line may still show
          there: select that cell to take it off. In a table with
          space between its cells every cell draws its own four
          lines, and the cells next to the selection are never
          changed. There is no line inside a cell merged across
          several cells. A line drawn only by the cell next door
          shows as off here.
  Table   the table's lines, which the cells show unless a cell
          has a line of its own, and the table's shading. The
          cells' own lines are kept, so Table with None does not
          take them off (a file from another program often gives
          every cell lines of its own).

Fill None takes off the cells' (or the table's) own shading; shading
a table style gives shows again, and is not shown in the box and
cannot be taken off by it. A side you turn off is written as "no
line". A side you tick for the first time starts with no gap
between the line and the cell's edge.

The bell sounds and the box stays open when a cell's or the table's
own lines or shading come from the file in a form !Letters can only
keep (a colour taken from a theme, say): change those in Word. A
line under the options says why the box refused. One undo step; the
selection stays.

Dragging the edges of columns and rows
--------------------------------------

Put the pointer on a vertical line between two cells (within 3
screen pixels, at any zoom) and drag with SELECT:

  - a dashed line shows where the border will go. The two columns
    beside it change and the table keeps its width. The line stops
    where a column would be narrower than 0.1 inch or wider than
    22 inches (a column already narrower than 0.1 inch is not
    widened).
  - from the table's right edge the last column changes, and so
    does the width of the table.
  - a row's bottom line dragged up or down (a dashed horizontal
    line) sets that row's height: At least the new height, or
    Exactly when the row was Exactly. It goes no higher than 22
    inches and no lower than 0.01 inch (so an Exactly row stays
    Exactly). A row dragged shorter than its text still shows its
    text (as Word does). Inside a cell merged down over the line
    no line is drawn, and no drag starts there.
  - a table that is as wide as its table style says (100% of the
    page, say) gets a width of its own when you drag its right
    edge, so the new width shows.
  - ESCAPE during the drag changes nothing. Letting go where you
    began changes nothing. If the document changes in the middle
    (the bell sounds) nothing is put in; closing the window in
    the middle ends the drag quietly.
  - each drag is one undo step. The widths are written in the file
    as shown: the columns and the cells over them, and the table's
    width when you drag its right edge.

A vertical line wins where the pointer is near both a vertical and
a horizontal line; in a table inside a cell the inner table's lines
come first, unless the outer line is nearer the pointer. The dashed
line for the right edge assumes the table's left edge stays where it
is: a centred table moves its edges by half as much each way, and a
right-aligned one keeps its right edge. No drag starts inside a cell
merged across the
line, at the table's left or top edge, or with SHIFT or CTRL held;
the press is first an ordinary click, which puts the caret in the
cell. In a table that is shown scaled (width Auto, or columns
!Letters could not use as written) a drag writes every column as it is
shown.

The ruler in a table
--------------------

With the caret in a cell, the ruler (section 5) shows the cell: its
left end is the cell's text area (inside the cell's margin), the
indent markers move the cell paragraph's indents, and the right
indent is measured from the right edge of the text area. There are
no column markers: use Table properties or drag the edges.

Typing and deleting in cells
----------------------------

  - Typing, Enter, Backspace and Delete over selected cells clear
    them: each cell keeps one empty paragraph, with the look of its
    first. The caret goes to the start of the top left cell.
  - Backspace over selected cells that span every column of the
    table (whole rows) removes those rows. Over only some columns
    it clears the cells, as Delete does. Backspace over the whole
    table deletes it; Delete over the whole table clears it.
  - Backspace and Delete at the start or end of a cell do nothing:
    they never join a cell to its neighbour, as in Word.
  - A selection from text into a table deletes the text and the
    rows it reaches (their cells' text and the rows themselves); a
    table the selection spans entirely goes whole. A selection from
    one table into another deletes the rows of each it reaches and
    what is between them, and leaves an empty paragraph between the
    two tables (the caret goes there), so that they stay two
    tables: Word would join two tables that touch. The same when
    the selection runs from just after one table to just before
    the next, in a cell too and for tables shown as grey boxes: a
    deletion never leaves two tables touching. A table shown as a
    grey box (above) keeps the old rule: Backspace or Delete next
    to it selects it, and a second press deletes it; but the empty
    paragraph between it and another table stays (the caret goes
    into it).
  - A cell is never left with nothing in it: it keeps an empty
    paragraph. A cell always ends with a paragraph, as Word needs:
    deleting leaves a cell that would end with a table with an
    empty paragraph after it, and Delete just after a table inside
    a cell never removes the cell's last paragraph (the caret goes
    into it instead).
  - Bookmarks in cells that are cleared stay if their other end is
    still in the document: a bookmark's start is put back at the
    start of the first cell cleared, its end at the end of the last
    one, so a bookmark never ends before it starts. Those of an
    empty paragraph that Backspace or Delete removes beside a table
    are put back at the caret (section 3). Bookmarks in rows that
    are removed move to the start of the next row (after the last
    row: to the end of the table), unless both ends were in those
    rows. A table deleted whole (Backspace over all its cells, or
    a selection that spans it) takes the bookmarks that are wholly
    inside it; a bookmark with only one end in it keeps that end,
    put back where the table was. A selection from a cell of one
    table into a cell of a later one can leave a bookmark that
    would end before it starts: its end is put just after its
    start instead (the bookmark is empty there), so Word keeps it.
  - CTRL-Enter (a page break) and a section break are refused in a
    cell, with a beep.
  - Each of these is one undo step.

Formatting, lists, links and the rest in cells
----------------------------------------------

All that works in text works in a cell, and on selected cells:

  - Character formatting (keys, Format menu, toolbar), Change case
    and the format painter. On selected cells they change the text
    of every cell of the rectangle, nested tables too; on a row or
    column, that row or column.
  - Paragraph formatting, spacing, tab stops, borders and shading of
    paragraphs, and the ruler's indents, the same way. A selection
    that starts in text and reaches into a table formats the rows
    it reaches whole.
  - Lists: making, changing and removing lists, the numbers shown,
    and typing "1. " or "- " (AutoFormat) all work in a cell. A list
    carries on through a table in reading order (section 7).
  - Links: Insert > Hyperlink in a cell, and a CTRL-click on a link
    there follows it as anywhere.
  - Bookmarks (Add, Go to, Delete) in a cell.
  - Symbols and special characters, and Insert > Picture, at the
    caret in a cell. A picture you put in a cell (from the Insert
    menu, by dropping a file or by pasting an image) is made no
    wider than the cell's text, as Word does, and saved at that
    size. A picture in a cell can be clicked, selected, resized by
    its handles and given alt text (section 9), as anywhere.
  - Find, Replace and Replace all search the text of cells, nested
    tables too (section 4). The word count counts a selection that
    ends in a cell as the selection, not the whole document, and
    counts the lines in cells.

Page break and Section break in the Insert menu are shaded while the
selection is in a cell.

Selecting and deleting a whole table
------------------------------------

A table with nothing beside it or a grey box is one thing in the
flow: the caret goes before or after it. Where there is a paragraph
beside it the caret goes straight into the cells.

  click on a grey box     selects the whole box
  copy, cut, paste        see the next sections

Info on the window's menu counts the tables, those inside tables
too ("8 paragraphs, 2 tables"), and the words of the text in
cells are counted in the word count.

Copying and pasting tables
--------------------------

CTRL-C copies what is selected, CTRL-X cuts it (a cut is a copy
that then deletes, as for text). What a table gives depends on the
selection:

  - selected cells: a table of those cells (a rectangle; a cell
    merged across or down comes whole, as the selection already
    takes it), with their widths, lines and shading
  - one or more whole rows (their row zone, or cells across the
    table): those rows, as a table
  - the whole table: the table as it is
  - text into a table, or out of one: the text, and for each table
    the rows the selection reaches, whole. A table the selection
    only touches at its far edge is not taken.

Where you paste a table, CTRL-V (one undo step) does this:

  - at the caret in text: in the middle of a paragraph the
    paragraph is split and the table goes between the halves; at
    its start the table goes before it, at its end after it. A
    table never touches another table: !Letters puts an empty
    paragraph between two (a table pasted at the start of the
    paragraph that follows a table, say, or two tables of a web
    page that come one after the other, in a cell too), as Word
    does.
  - at the caret in a cell: the table goes in the cell as a
    nested table (tables can be nested eight deep). Pasted where
    it would be deeper than that, it comes as text instead: a
    paragraph for each row, its cells separated by tabs.
  - over selected cells: when what you paste is one table, its
    cells overwrite the selected ones, from the top left of the
    selection, cell for cell. The cells keep their own width,
    lines and shading; only what is in them changes. Pasted cells
    beyond the selection are not used, and a cell that is the
    hidden lower part of a merged cell gives nothing and takes
    nothing. The caret goes to the end of the last cell
    overwritten. A bookmark that starts in a cell overwritten and
    ends outside the cells keeps its start at the start of the
    first cell overwritten; one that ends there keeps its end at
    the end of the last, so it still starts before it ends.
    Anything else pasted over selected cells clears them first, as
    typing over them does.

What comes with a table:

  - Its lists, pictures and links come by the rules of sections 4,
    7 and 9: numbers of their own, a copy of each picture file in
    another document, links as text there. A bookmark, comment or
    tracked change stays with the original.
  - Into another document the table style comes too (Grid Table
    4, say), with the styles it is based on, so the table looks
    as it did. If that document has a table style of that name
    (capitals and small letters alike) it is used as it is; if
    the name belongs there to a style of another kind, the table
    gets the document's ordinary table look instead. One paste
    brings at most 500 table styles; past that, a table gets the
    ordinary look.
  - A content control in a cell (a box Word uses in forms), or
    anything else !Letters keeps as it was without showing it, is
    left out when the table goes into another document, as such
    things are outside tables; in the same document it comes.
  - A pasted table is a copy: nothing you do to it changes the
    original.

Tables and other programs
-------------------------

A table you copy reaches other programs in two forms, and the
program takes the one it prefers.

  - As a web page table (a spreadsheet, Word, Google Docs, a mail
    or a web page editor take this): rows and cells, merged
    cells (colspan and rowspan), column widths, shading, and a
    thin black line on the cells that have a line. The look of a
    table style that is not on a cell itself (a shaded header
    row, banded rows) is not in it, so such a table can look
    plainer there than in !Letters.
  - As text (Notepad, a terminal): a line for each row, its cells
    separated by tabs; a cell's paragraphs, and a table inside a
    cell, are joined by spaces.

Pasting a table from another program makes a real table in !Letters.
This works for the tables of a web page, Word, Google Docs,
LibreOffice and spreadsheets such as Excel:

  - Merged cells, shading, the widths the program gives and the
    lines it draws (as thin black lines on all four sides of the
    cell) come. A header cell (a th) is bold. A table caption
    comes as a paragraph above the table. The table has no table
    style.
  - A column with no width of its own shares what is left of the
    table's width equally, and is at least a quarter of an inch
    (the table's width is its own when the program gives one, else
    that of the text at the caret: a cell's width in a cell).
  - Anything in the web page that could run or fetch something is
    left out, as for any paste (section 19).
  - These limits keep a paste manageable: 63 columns, 1000 rows,
    tables eight deep and 63,000 cells in all. A table beyond
    them, or one with anything else between its rows, comes as
    text, a paragraph for each row with tabs between the cells,
    and never cut short.

Converting text to a table and back
-----------------------------------

Table > Convert > Text to table... turns the selected paragraphs
into a table. Select the paragraphs first (part of a paragraph
counts as all of it; a selection that stops at the very start of a
paragraph leaves that one out), then choose it. The box has:

  Columns        how many columns; !Letters works it out when the box
                 opens and again when you change the separator
  Separate text  Paragraphs: each paragraph is a cell, filling
  at             rows Columns cells at a time (the last row is
                 filled out with empty cells)
                 Tabs, Commas: each paragraph is a row, cut at its
                 tabs or commas
                 Other: each paragraph is a row, cut at the one
                 character you type beside it

!Letters starts with Tabs when every paragraph has a tab, else Commas
when every one has a comma, else Paragraphs. A row with fewer
pieces than Columns gets empty cells; one with more keeps its
extra pieces, with their separators, in its last cell, so no text
is lost. The separators themselves go. The table is the one
Insert > Table... makes (a line round every cell, equal columns
across the text), each cell keeping the paragraph look and the
character formatting of its text; pictures, links and bookmarks go
with their text. The caret goes in the first cell. It is one undo
step.

The command is shaded with no selection, a caret alone, or a
selection that reaches into a table or a grey box or runs over two
sections. In the box, OK refuses (the bell and a message) to cut a
field (a table of contents, a page number, a cross-reference) into
cells: a field with a separator inside it, a field that runs over
more than one paragraph (a table of contents, whatever the
separator, Paragraphs too), and a selection that starts or ends
inside a field. Columns must be 1 to 63 and
Other one character you can type (not a control character, and not
a space: Word allows a space); more than 32767 rows is refused. An
empty cell (a short row's, or an empty piece between two
separators) is not in a list, even when its paragraph was: it
shows no number.

Table > Convert > Table to text... turns a table into paragraphs.
Put the caret anywhere in it (in a nested table, the nested table
is the one converted). The whole table is converted, not only the
selected rows (Word converts the selected rows). The box offers
Paragraph marks, Tabs, Commas and Other for the separator.

  - Each row becomes a paragraph: the text of its cells, one after
    the other, with the separator between. A cell's paragraphs stay
    together and in order: a cell's second paragraph starts a new
    paragraph, and the next cell's text carries on in it (cells
    "a1 / a2" and "b" give "a1", then "a2", the separator and
    "b"). So the text keeps the order of the table. With Paragraph
    marks every paragraph of every cell is a paragraph of its own.
  - A table inside a cell is converted first, in the same way, and
    its paragraphs come where it stood. A merged cell counts once.
    Anything in the hidden lower part of a cell merged down (Word
    does not show it) is not lost: it comes as paragraphs of its
    own where it stood.
  - Bookmarks stay, in order; the converted paragraphs are
    selected, and one CTRL-Z gives the table back.

Both boxes close with Cancel or Escape, which change nothing;
RETURN is OK. If you moved the caret or changed the document while
the box was open, the first OK beeps, says so and fills the box
again for the new selection; click it again. Choosing Text to
table... again for other paragraphs while the box is open fills
it for those.

Keys in tables, at a glance
---------------------------

  Tab               next cell (a new row after the last cell)
  Shift-Tab         previous cell
  CTRL-Tab          a tab character in the cell (or the Tab item of
                    the Table and Insert menus, as some browsers
                    keep CTRL-Tab for themselves)
  Enter             a new paragraph in the cell
  CTRL-SHIFT-Enter  splits the table at the caret's row
  Shift-click, Shift-arrows   select a rectangle of cells
  CTRL-A            select all: a table that is all there is is
                    taken whole
  Backspace, Delete over selected cells   clear them (Backspace
                    removes whole rows, as above)
  CTRL-C, CTRL-X, CTRL-V   copy, cut and paste cells, rows, tables
  CTRL-Z            undo: every table command is one step
  ESCAPE            closes a box; during a drag, changes nothing

Limits
------

  - 63 columns in a table, and 63 grid columns when a file is read;
    a command that would make more beeps (the Insert table, Split
    cells and Convert boxes also say why)
  - Insert table: 1 to 1000 rows (a table may have up to 32767 rows,
    which splitting cells and converting text to a table can make)
  - tables inside tables eight deep; a table in a cell of the eighth
    is not drawn but kept (a grey box), and Insert table, a paste and
    a conversion that would go deeper are refused, or come as text
  - a pasted web page table: 63 columns, 1000 rows, 8 deep, 63,000
    cells; beyond that it comes as text
  - widths 0.02 to 22 inches (a column dragged to 0.1 inch at the
    least); row heights 0 to 22 inches; a row is drawn at most 22
    inches high when it asks for a height, but grows for its text
  - a table is drawn at most 4000 pixels wide, a column at least a
    pixel
  - a document may hold up to a million paragraphs, those in cells
    included

What is kept, untouched, in a table
-----------------------------------

A table !Letters draws is saved back as it was, but for the changes you
make. Settings it does not show or does not know are kept where they
were and written in the order Word needs: a style's conditional
formatting, repeated header rows, a floating table's place, the
directions of text, row and cell settings of Word's later versions,
the numbers Word uses to tell its saves apart. A command changes only
what it names: setting a row's height leaves its cells alone. A
table the program cannot read completely (see "A grey box instead")
is not touched at all, to the byte. In a Strict Word file the table
is written with that form's own names (start and end for left and
right), also for the tables you make there.

Not possible yet
----------------

  - Moving a floating table with the mouse: its edges can be
    dragged as in any table, but its place on the page stays where
    the document puts it (section 13). A floating table in a cell,
    a header or a footer stays in the text, with a dashed outline
  - Tracked changes to a table (a row inserted or a width changed
    with Track changes on, in Word): such a table is a grey box.
    !Letters has no Track changes switch, so what you change in a
    table is never tracked. Tracked changes in the text of cells
    are shown as in any paragraph
  - Editing a table style, or making one: a table style can be
    used, copied with its table into another document, and shown,
    not changed
  - Content controls round whole rows or cells: the table is a grey
    box
  - AutoFit commands, sorting, formulas, the other commands of
    Word's Table Design and Layout tabs, text direction and
    right-to-left tables (drawn left to right)
  - Find, Replace and Go to can reach the text hidden in the lower
    part of a cell merged down, which nobody sees (Word does not
    show it either)
  - Insert > Bookmark... with cells selected marks from the first
    selected cell to the last in reading order, so it also takes
    the cells between them outside the rectangle (Word marks the
    rectangle's columns only)

Things to know about tables on screen:

  - At some zooms (50%, 75%, 110%, 150%...) a thin light line can
    show between shaded cells; at 100% and 200% there is none.
  - Below 100% the thinnest lines (half a point) are drawn grey,
    not black, as paragraph borders are.
  - In a row cut off at Exactly, the caret and the selection
    shading are not cut off: in the hidden part of a cell's text
    they show below the row, over the next one.
  - The zones that select a cell, a row or a column (just inside a
    cell's left edge, left of a row, above a column) are a fixed
    size on the page: very narrow at 50%, wide at 400%.
  - A press just right of a line between cells is first a click in
    that cell, so the cell's text stays selected after the drag.
  - A table inside a cell that is wider than the cell cannot have
    its edges dragged outside the outer cell.

Still to be checked in real Word, for the clipboard and Convert:
that a table pasted over cells does what Word's Overwrite cells
does (!Letters keeps the cells' own lines and widths, and leaves the
hidden part of a merged cell alone) and where Word puts the caret
afterwards; what Word does with a table pasted eight tables deep
(!Letters pastes text); what Word does when a table style it copies
has the same name as another kind of style; that Word keeps two
touching tables apart as !Letters does (an empty paragraph); how
Word reads the web page !Letters puts on the clipboard (widths in
points, the thin black lines, no banded rows or header shading)
and how !Letters reads Word's, Google Docs' and Excel's (widths, any
kind of line as a thin one, merged cells); that Word's Convert
agrees with !Letters on whole table against selected rows, on extra
pieces kept in the last cell, on a field that a separator would
cut (!Letters refuses, also for a field over several paragraphs), on
the format a separator takes, on a cell's paragraphs kept together
in Table to text, on tables nested in the table converted, on the
hidden part of a merged cell (!Letters keeps what is in it) and on
empty cells of a list (!Letters does not number
them); where Word keeps a bookmark that starts or ends in cells
pasted over or cleared; and what Word brings of a content control
in a cell pasted into another document (!Letters leaves it out).

Still to be checked in real Word, for the Table menu: that a table
made with Insert table... opens in Word with the Table Grid look and
no repair message; what Word copies from the caret's paragraph into
the cells; what widths Word gives Fixed columns; the places the
new column lines go when a cell is split into more columns than it
spans (!Letters keeps the old lines and adds equal ones); that Word
also keeps the hidden text of a cell merged down when you merge
across it; that a row added below a cell merged down to the last
row (Tab in the last cell) gets a cell of its own in Word too; what
Word does with columns added to a table whose widths are percentages
(!Letters gives the new cells widths in inches); that rows above a
header row stay header rows; that Merge cells and Split cells put
paragraphs and bookmarks where Word does; and that a table split in
two reads as two tables in Word.

Still to be checked in real Word, for editing: what Word does with
the bookmarks of cleared cells (!Letters puts them back at the caret)
and of deleted rows (!Letters moves them to the next row); whether a
row added below a table's header rows repeats as a header (!Letters:
only between two header rows); which paragraphs Word formats
when a selection runs from text into a table (!Letters takes the
rows it reaches whole); where bookmarks that sit between rows or
cells are shown; whether Word sizes a picture put in a cell to
exactly the cell's text width; and CTRL-Tab, which a browser may
not pass on.

Still to be checked in real Word, for formatting: how Word writes
and reads back what !Letters writes for a table's width, alignment and
indent (a negative indent, left and right alignment); the row
heights Exactly and At least; a cell's width without a change of
the table's columns; whether a column width on a table with a
width and Word's automatic columns holds; the vertical alignment
of a cell merged down; the lines of a cell and its neighbours when
a line is turned off ("no line" on both: Word may write another
form), and what Word does with a wider neighbour; whether Word shows
a line drawn only by the cell next door as on; whether Apply to
Table in Word takes off the cells' own lines; a table's width when
its right edge is dragged (!Letters turns a percentage width into
inches, also one the table style gives); how little a column may be
dragged to (Word's limit is smaller than !Letters' 0.1 inch); and the
minimum width of 0.02 inch.

Still to be checked in real Word, for drawing: how closely the
lines and shading match Word's for the more unusual styles of
line; the widths of tables that let Word work their columns out
from the text (!Letters estimates these when a file's own widths
cannot be right); which cell's line wins where a cell's own border
meets one from the table's style; and the exact colours of the
theme colours.


11) Pages and page setup
------------------------

!Letters shows a document as pages, as Word does, and lets you set up
those pages: their paper, orientation and margins, and how each
section of the document starts.

Print layout and Draft
----------------------

Print layout, the view most documents open in, shows the pages one
below the other, each a white sheet of the document's own paper
(A4, Letter, a landscape page...), with grey desk between them and
small grey marks at the corners of the area the text goes in.
Draft, on the View menu, shows the same text on one long page with
no page edges: it is the quickest view to type in, and every break
is drawn as a labelled dotted line (section 8). Web layout and
Outline are on the View menu too (section 2). The text can be
edited in every view (the keys and the menus are the same; Outline
adds its own, section 2): switching keeps the selection, and the
caret's line stays where it was in the window.

Which view a window opens in:

  - the view the file was saved in, if Word saved it in Print
    layout, Web layout, Outline or Draft (Word calls Draft
    "Normal"; its Master Document view opens in Outline)
  - otherwise the view you chose last, in any window (it is kept in
    Choices:Letters, so it is the same the next time !Letters starts)
  - otherwise Print layout

The view is never saved in the file: a document saved in Print
layout is the same, to the byte, as one saved in Draft.

The box at the right-hand end of the toolbar's second row says
where the caret is: 'Page 3 of 12', and '(Section 2)' after it when
the document has more than one section. A long document's pages
are worked out a few at a time: while that goes on the count ends
in '...' ('Page 3 of 12...'), and it grows until every page is
counted. You can type and scroll in the meantime. In Draft, Web
layout and Outline the box shows only the section.

Moving about the pages:

  click in the text          the caret goes there, as always
  click in a top or bottom   the first or last line of that page, at
    margin                   the place clicked
  click in the grey desk     the nearer page's first or last line
  click in the header or     nothing; a double-click there edits
    footer band              the header or footer (section 12)
  Up, Down                   from the last line of a page to the
                             first of the next, and back
  CTRL-Page Down             the first line of the next page
  CTRL-Page Up               the first line of the previous page
  drag past the window       selects on across the pages

A web browser may keep CTRL-Page Down and CTRL-Page Up for itself
(to change its own tabs). Next page and Previous page on the Edit
menu do the same as the keys. In Draft the keys and the two items
move a window's height, as Page Down and Page Up do.

The band for a header is the strip from the header's distance from
the top of the page (Format > Layout..., below) down to where the
text starts; the footer's is the same at the bottom. Above and below
those bands is margin.

Zoom (section 5) works in all four views, and the ruler shows the
page the caret is on: a landscape page's ruler is wider.

Where the pages break
---------------------

!Letters fills each page with whole lines, as Word does, and starts a
new page:

  - after a page break (CTRL-Enter: section 8)
  - before a paragraph with Page break before (section 6), unless it
    is already the first thing on its page
  - at a section break that starts a new page: Next page, Even page
    and Odd page (section 8). A Continuous break does not, unless
    the new section has another paper size or orientation. A New
    column break starts the next column of a page set in columns,
    else a new page (section 13)
  - where the page is full

When a page is full, the options of the Paragraph box (section 6)
are followed:

  Widow/orphan control  a paragraph's first line is never left alone
                        at the bottom of a page, nor its last line
                        alone at the top: one more line goes over
                        with it. It is on unless a paragraph or its
                        style turns it off, as in Word
  Keep lines together   the whole paragraph goes to the next page
                        (unless it is longer than a page)
  Keep with next        the paragraph goes to the next page with the
                        first line of the one after it: a heading is
                        never left at the bottom of a page. Several
                        in a row go together, but a run longer than
                        a page is broken as usual

A line taller than a whole page, or a picture that is, is cut in two
at the bottom of the page and carries on at the top of the next.

The space before a paragraph is left out at the top of a page when
the page broke on its own, and kept after a page break, a section
break or Page break before, as in Word 2013 and later.

Even page and Odd page breaks start the new section on the next
page with an even or an odd number. If the page after the break has
the wrong number (an Odd page break on page 1 would give page 2),
Word prints a blank page between them. !Letters counts that page,
so the section starts on page 3 and the box says 'Page 3 of ...',
but it does not draw it: the empty page is not shown.

Each page is drawn at its own section's size: a landscape section
in a portrait document has landscape pages. Vertical alignment
(Format > Layout...) Centre or Bottom moves the text down the last
page of the section, to its middle or its foot.

Tables across pages
-------------------

A table that fits on its page stays whole. A longer one carries on
on the next page, breaking between its rows. A row that does not
fit is split: each of its cells carries on, line by line, at the top
of the next page, and the row's lines and shading are drawn round
each part (the edge where it is cut is drawn too). A cell whose text
is centred or at the bottom of the cell is drawn from the top of
each part of a split row.

  - A row set in Word not to break across pages goes whole to the
    next page, unless it is taller than a page. A row with an exact
    height is never split.
  - A row with a height of "at least" so much, whose text fits on the
    page but whose height does not, goes whole to the next page; if
    it is already the first on its page, it is cut short after its
    text (the rest of its height is left out).
  - Header rows (rows set in Word to repeat as a header row, at the
    top of the table) are drawn again at the top of each page the
    table carries on to, unless they and the row after them do not
    fit. They are not text of their own: a click in a repeated
    header row puts the caret in the header row itself, on the
    table's first page.
  - Cells merged down over several rows carry on with their rows: the
    part of the merged cell beside each row is on that row's page.
  - A table inside a cell is kept whole on one page; one taller than
    a page is cut between its rows.
  - A table whose every row is set to Keep with next goes to the next
    page whole, with the paragraph after it.

The caret, clicks, selections, CTRL-Page Down and Next page all work
in the parts of a table on every page.

Lines round paragraphs (Format > Borders and shading...) that are
cut by the end of a page are closed: the box ends at the foot of the
first page and starts again at the top of the next.

Page setup
----------

Format > Page setup... opens a box for the page:

  Paper            A4, A5, A3, Letter, Legal, Executive, B5 (JIS),
                   or Custom
  Width, Height    the size of the paper (1 to 22 inches); choosing
                   a paper fills them in, and typing a size shows
                   its paper (to the hundredth of an inch, as the
                   box shows it: 8.27" by 11.69" is A4), or Custom
  Orientation      Portrait or Landscape (it swaps the width and
                   height)
  Top, Bottom      the margins at the top and foot of the page (a
                   minus sign, as -1", makes the margin exact: in
                   Word a tall header then never pushes the text
                   down)
  Left, Right      the margins at the sides
  Gutter           an extra margin for binding
  Gutter position  Left or Top (this is for the whole document; it
                   is shaded for the rare file with no settings of
                   its own)
  Apply to         This section, This point forward or Whole
                   document

Lengths are in inches; type 36 pt for points. Apply to starts at
This section when the document has more than one section, else at
Whole document. A value the sections chosen do not share is left
blank, and stays as each section has it unless you type one. This
point forward puts a Next page section break at the caret (as
Insert > Section break does: a paragraph is split there) and sets up
the pages from there to the end; at the very start of a section
nothing is put in.

A length field takes only figures, a point, a minus sign, spaces,
" and the letters of in and pt: other keys put nothing in. A field
you empty keeps the value it had, as a blank one does.

OK (or Return) changes only what you changed in the box, as one step
that CTRL-Z undoes. A value that is still not a length (such as
1.2.3), a size outside 1 to 22 inches, a margin over 22 inches, or
margins that would leave less than a tenth of an inch for the text
is not rounded: the bell sounds, the box says why and stays open,
with the caret in the field. This point forward is refused in a
table's cell. If the selection or the document changed while the box
was open, OK fills the box again from the new selection and says so.
Cancel, Escape and the close icon change nothing.

The Layout box
--------------

Format > Layout... sets up the section the caret is in (with a
selection, the section where it starts):

  Section start           how the section starts: New page,
                          Continuous, Even page, Odd page or New
                          column (as the section breaks of
                          section 8)
  Different odd and even  odd and even pages have headers and
                          footers of their own (for the whole
                          document; shaded, like the Gutter
                          position, for the rare file with no
                          settings of its own)
  Different first page    the section's first page has a header and
                          footer of its own
  From edge: header,      how far the header is from the top of the
    Footer                page, and the footer from the foot (0 to
                          22 inches)
  Vertical alignment      Top, Centre, Justified or Bottom: where the
                          text goes down the page when it does not
                          fill it
  Line numbers...         opens the Line numbers box for this
                          section (section 13)

OK, Cancel, the bell and the messages work as in Page setup.
Different first page, Different odd and even and the distances
change the headers and footers the pages show at once (section 12,
where the Header menu sets the first two as well). Justified is
saved but drawn as Top for now.

How close to Word
-----------------

Word decides where its pages break as it goes, with its own fonts,
and it does not say how. !Letters follows the same rules, but its
pages are not always Word's: a page may start in another place than
Word's, and once one page differs the pages after it usually do
too, so a long document can have a page more or less. Exact
equality is not promised; where they differ, Word's pages are the
ones that count.

How close? Word records in a file where its pages (and its
columns) began when it last saved it. Over the sample documents
!Letters is tested with, a little over four in ten of those recorded
page starts match a page or column start of !Letters within a line
today. Pages that start at a page break, a section break or Page
break before almost always match, and about half of the column
starts do. Pages that break on their own mostly do not: about seven
in ten of them start more than a line away from Word's, often
several. A line a little taller or shorter early on moves every page
after it.
Headers and footers are on the pages and push the text as in Word
(section 12), though how tall Word makes them is still to be
checked. Columns, line numbers, and floating pictures and tables
with the text flowing round them are on the pages too (section 13),
each with its own differences from Word. Not on the pages yet:

  - footnotes, which Word puts at the foot of their page
  - floating pictures and tables in headers and footers: they stay
    in the header's text

The fonts are stand-ins with the same widths as Word's (section 17),
so most lines break as Word's do, but not all. Other things done in
a simpler way than Word:

  - Vertical alignment moves only the last page of a section; Word
    may move every page of it. Justified is drawn as Top.
  - Where cells have space between them, the table's outline is not
    closed where a page cuts it.
  - The top line of a box round paragraphs that carries on from the
    page before is drawn just above its first line, in the margin.
  - The blank page an Even or Odd page break needs is counted but
    not drawn.

There is no printing yet: Print layout shows how the pages will be.


12) Headers, footers and fields
-------------------------------

In Print layout every page shows its header at the top and its
footer at the foot, as Word prints them: their text, tables,
pictures, page numbers and dates. Draft, Web layout and Outline show
the text only, without them. Headers and footers you have not
changed are saved exactly as they were.

A floating picture or table in a header or footer (a letterhead's
logo, often) is not placed on the page as Word places it: it is
shown in the header's own text, where it is anchored, with a dashed
outline, and the body's text does not flow round it. It is kept as
it is, and Word shows it in its place.

What the pages show
-------------------

A header sits at its distance from the top of the page, and a
footer at its distance from the foot (Format > Layout..., section
11). A header taller than the space above the text pushes the text
down, and a tall footer pushes it up, so fewer lines fit on that
page, as in Word; with an exact top or bottom margin (a minus sign
in Page setup) the text is never pushed. A header !Letters cannot
read is kept exactly as it was and shown as a grey band,
"[header - preserved, not shown]"; it pushes nothing.

Which header a page shows depends on its section's settings:

  Different first page  the section's first page shows a header and
                        footer of its own (an empty one if there is
                        none)
  Different odd and     pages with an even number show headers and
    even pages          footers of their own (for the whole
                        document)
  otherwise             the section's header and footer

A section with no header of its own shows the one of the section
before it: in Word's words it is "linked to the previous" section,
and !Letters labels it "Same as previous". The first section has
nothing before it: without a header of its own, its pages show none.

A Continuous section break that starts a section part way down a
page does not give the new section a first page: that page shows
the header, footer and page number of the section before, the next
page shows the new section's ordinary header (not its first-page
one), and its page numbers carry on rather than starting again. This
is still to be checked in real Word.

Editing a header or footer
--------------------------

Double-click in the band where a page's header goes (between the top
of the page's text and the header's distance from the edge), or the
band at the foot for the footer; or choose View > Header and footer
for the header of the page the caret is on. Double-clicking works
in Print layout, where the bands are; View > Header and footer works
from any view: the window changes to Print layout first. A single
click or a drag in the band, outside the mode, does nothing.

The window is then in the header and footer mode: the body of every
page is drawn pale, a dashed blue line marks where the header ends
(and where the footer begins), and a tab names what you are
editing: Header, Footer, First page header, Even page footer, Odd
page header..., with the section ("- Section 2") when there are
several, and "Same as previous" when it is linked. The box at the
end of the toolbar's second row says 'Page 2 of 4 - Header'.

Everything you do in the text works in a header or footer too:
typing, deleting, undo, selecting, formatting, lists, the ruler,
tables, pictures, links, symbols and fields, copy and paste. What a
header cannot hold is refused: page, column and section breaks are
shaded on the Insert menu and their keys beep. Format > Page
setup..., Format > Layout... and Format > Columns... are shaded
while you are in the mode. If one of them (or Line numbers...) was
already open when you went into the mode, its OK beeps and the box
says to leave the header or footer first; it stays open, and OK
works again once you are back in the text.

A header shows on many pages, but it is one piece of text: a click
in it on any page edits it there, and the caret follows to that
page. Moving about:

  click or drag in the        works as in the text
    same header or footer
  click in another header     goes to that one
    or footer
  double-click in the body    back to the text, the caret where you
                              clicked
  Escape, or View > Header    back to the text, the caret where it
    and footer again          was

A page that shows no header (or no first-page or even-page header,
when the section has one of those turned on) can be double-clicked
all the same. Nothing is made until you type: the first key you
press makes the header, in Word's Header style (as in Word's own
template: a centre tab stop at 3.13 inches and a right one at 6.27
inches, the middle and right of the text on A4 paper with one-inch
margins, wherever the margins are), and that and
what you type are one step that CTRL-Z undoes. Leaving without
typing changes nothing.

Typing in a header that is "Same as previous" changes that one
header, so every section linked to it changes too, as in Word. If no
section had a header at all, the new one is made for the first
section of the chain and shown by all of them. To give one section a
header of its own, untick Link to previous first (below).

The Header menu
---------------

In the mode the window's menu has a Header item (or Footer, in a
footer) after Outline; View > Header and footer options is the same
menu. Each item is one step that CTRL-Z undoes:

  Different first page  the section's first page gets a header and
                        footer of its own (ticked while on)
  Different odd and     even pages get headers and footers of their
    even pages          own, in the whole document (ticked while on;
                        the rare file with no settings part beeps)
  Link to previous      ticked when the section shows the section
                        before's header; shaded in the first section
  Go to footer          the footer (or header) of the same page
    (Go to header)
  Previous section,     the same kind of header on the first page of
    Next section        the section before or after
  Remove header         empties the header (in every section that
    (Remove footer)     shows it); shaded for one not made yet

Untick Link to previous and the section gets a copy of the header it
was showing, to change on its own (with nothing to copy, an empty
one). Tick it and the section's own header is deleted, and its file
in the document too when no other section uses it: the section then
shows the one before. Word asks before it deletes a header this way;
!Letters does not ask, but CTRL-Z puts the header back. After each
item the mode stays on the same page, on whatever header or footer
the page shows now.

Undo, and finding text
----------------------

CTRL-Z and redo take the window to the change they undo or redo. Undo
a change made in a header while you are in the body, and the window
goes into that header, on the first page that shows it from the
caret's page on; undo a change to the body while you are in a
header, and the window goes back to the body. Undoing the step that
made a header leaves the mode, since that header is gone. In Draft
(which shows no headers) the window stays in the body.

In the mode Find, Find next, Replace and Replace all look in the
header or footer you are editing only, never in the body or the
other headers. Outside the mode they look in the body only.

Fields
------

A field is a piece of text that Word works out for itself, such as a
page number or today's date. The file keeps each field's code (PAGE,
DATE...) and the result Word last worked out. !Letters works out
these on the screen as you look:

  PAGE                 the number of the page it is on, in the
                       numbering the section asks for (1, i, A...)
  NUMPAGES             how many pages the document has
  SECTIONPAGES         how many pages its section has
  SECTION              the number of its section
  DATE, TIME           the date and time the window was opened,
                       or when you last put in or updated a field
                       there (Insert, Update field, the Field box)

The page fields are worked out in Print layout. Draft shows the
values last worked out there, or the result in the file. While a
long document's pages are still being counted, NUMPAGES shows the
count from before, and the new one as soon as all are counted. A
page field in a table's cell shows the result in the file.

Every other field shows the result in the file until you update it
(below): AUTHOR, TITLE, FILENAME, NUMWORDS, CREATEDATE, SAVEDATE,
PRINTDATE and the like. Some are never worked out at all, only kept
and shown as they were: a table of contents, a cross-reference
(PAGEREF, REF), a mail merge field, IF, a formula, INCLUDETEXT and
INCLUDEPICTURE and the rest. So is a locked field, a field whose
result runs over several paragraphs, and a field inside a link, a
content control or a tracked insertion: !Letters shows and keeps
such a field as it is and never works it out. You can type in its
result as in other text, but deleting or replacing part of its
hidden marks takes the whole field (below).

A field worked out on the screen is one piece:

  - Left and Right step over it in one press, and a click on it puts
    the caret just before it or just after it, whichever is nearer.
  - Text typed next to it goes before or after the whole field.
  - Backspace or Delete next to it selects its result; a second
    press beeps. To delete the whole field, select from just before
    it to just after it (SHIFT-Right from before it) and press
    Delete.
  - You cannot type into its result, or paste or insert over part of
    it: the computer beeps. Word lets you change the result, and
    puts it back at the next update.

A field is never left in halves: a selection that takes in part of a
field's hidden marks (its start, its end, or the mark between its
code and its result) takes the whole field when you delete it, type
over it, cut it or paste over it, as in Word. Copy copies just what
is selected; when such a copy is pasted, the halves of fields in it
are left out, so a broken field never gets into a document.

Edit > Update field and Edit > Update all fields put a field's value
into the document itself (section 2 says which fields each takes; in
the header and footer mode Update field takes every field of the
header, with its page's number, and Update all fields is shaded). On
the screen a page number is worked out as you look, but the file
keeps the result it was saved with until you update it: saving never
updates a field. A page field is updated with the number of the page
it is on once all the pages are counted. Neither item has a key (F9
stays Redo, as in !Edit).

Insert > Page number, Number of pages, Date, Time and Field... put a
field in (section 8): in a footer, a page number shows each page's
own number.

Field codes and shading
-----------------------

View > Field codes (or SHIFT-F9) shows each field's code, such as
{ PAGE }, in place of its result, in the text and in the headers and
footers; choose it again for the results. It changes only what is
drawn: the document, and the file you save, stay as they were. With
the codes shown the caret steps over a code as over a result. A
field inside a link, a content control or a tracked insertion, or
one whose result runs over several paragraphs, still shows its
result.

View > Field shading draws a light grey behind field results: Never,
When selected (as it starts: while the caret is in the field or next
to it) or Always. The choice is kept for next time and is the same
in every window. The same fields as above are never shaded.

Still to be checked in real Word, and not possible yet
-------------------------------------------------------

Still to be checked: how tall Word makes a header (its Header style
and the space after it), and so where a page with a tall header
breaks; the first page of a Continuous section (above); a date or
time with no format of its own (!Letters shows 14/10/2026 and 14:05;
CREATEDATE, SAVEDATE and PRINTDATE as 14/10/2026 14:05); and the
odd number formats (a roman 0, say).

Not possible yet: headers and footers in Draft; a header's height
worked out with the page numbers it shows (pages are broken with
the result in the file, which only matters when a page number is so
long that the header takes another line); a page field in a table
cell worked out; a question before Link to previous deletes a
header (CTRL-Z puts it back).


13) Columns, line numbers and text wrap
---------------------------------------

Columns
-------

In Print layout a section set out in two or more columns of text
(as newspapers are) has its columns side by side on each page: the
text fills the first column, then the next, and after the last
column goes on at the top of the next page. A column break
(CTRL-SHIFT-Enter outside a table, or Insert > Column break: section
8) starts the next column, and so does a section that starts
with New column (Format > Layout..., section 11); after the last
column of a page either starts the next page. A page break ends the
page wherever it is.

Columns need not all be as wide. Each paragraph is set at the width
of the column it starts in, so its lines fit that column; one that
runs on from one column into a column of another width keeps the
lines it was given in the first (Word sets each part at its own
column's width). Setting a paragraph at another width moves every
paragraph after it, so !Letters works through the pages in order:
the pages up to the one with the caret at once, the rest a moment
later, page by page. In a long document with columns of different
widths the pages further on may change for a few seconds after you
open it or type a line; they end up the same however you got there.
Now and then a paragraph at the top of a wide column is set at the
narrower width of the column before it, when it would only fit that
column at the wider one. On a page of very narrow, very uneven
columns where a paragraph keeps moving between columns, !Letters
stops trying and leaves the page as it is.

Draft shows a section in columns as one column, at the width of its
first column, as Word's Draft view does. Click, select and type in
any column as anywhere else: Up and Down go from the foot of one
column to the top of the next.

Before a Continuous section break the columns are balanced: the
text of the section's last page is shared out so the columns end at
about the same height (the first may be a line longer). The section
after the break starts on the same page, below the longest column,
with its own columns (one, or as many as it has). At the end of the
document the columns are not balanced, as in Word: the last page
fills the first column first. To balance them there, put a
Continuous section break after the last paragraph in Word.

A section whose columns have a line between them (Line between in
the Columns box) shows a thin black line in the middle of the space
between each two columns that have text, as long as the longest of
those columns: when a Continuous break puts other columns below them
on the same page, each set of columns has its own lines.

Columns are shown as the document sets them out, including documents
saved in Strict form, which give the spacing in points or inches
(such as 18pt); they are written back the same way. A document whose
columns are so many or so wide that they would not fit the page has
them drawn squeezed onto it.

The Columns box
---------------

Format > Columns... opens the Columns box for the section the caret
is in. It shows the section's columns now:

  Presets       One, Two or Three columns of the same width; Left
                or Right: two columns, a narrow one (on the left or
                on the right) and a wide one, as Word makes them
  Number of columns  1 to 45
  Equal column width  every column as wide as the others; when it
                is on, only the first Spacing can be changed and the
                widths follow it
  Column 1, 2, 3  the width of each column and the space after it
                (the last column has no space after it); the box
                shows three: more than three columns you set here
                are all the same width (uneven columns past the
                third that a document already has are kept)
  Line between  a thin line down the middle of each space between
                columns
  Apply to      This section, This point forward (a Continuous
                section break goes in at the caret, and the columns
                start there) or Whole document

Click a preset and its values go into the box. Click OK (or press
RETURN) to set the columns; CTRL-Z puts them back as one step.
Cancel or ESCAPE changes nothing. When the document has more than
one section, This section is chosen first. With Whole document or
This point forward over sections that differ, a value they do not
share (the number of columns, say) is blank: leave it blank and each
section keeps its own, so you can, for example, tick Line between
for them all and keep their numbers of columns.

Some values are refused, with a beep and a line in the box saying
why: a number of columns that is not 1 to 45, a column narrower
than half an inch (too many columns, or too much space between
them, for the page), or widths and spaces that add up to more than
the width of the text. These are checked only when you change the
columns: Line between alone is taken even when the document's own
columns are narrower than that. Change them and click OK again.
Columns written in a way !Letters does not understand are kept just
as they are, and the box refuses to change them ("These columns
cannot be changed here"): change them in Word. If you move
the caret or change the document while the box is open, OK beeps
and the box shows the columns again as they are now: look at them,
then click OK.

Line numbers
------------

A Word document can number the lines of a section down the left
margin, as legal and draft documents often do. In Print layout
!Letters shows these numbers as Word does: to the left of each line
of text, a quarter of an inch from it (or the distance the document
gives), in the document's Line Number style if it has one, else in
the Normal style's font. Draft, Web layout and Outline show no line
numbers, as in Word.

What is numbered: every line of the section's paragraphs, empty
paragraphs too. Lines in tables, in headers and footers, and in a
paragraph with Suppress line numbers (Format > Paragraph..., or in
its style) are not numbered and not counted. The document says
whether the numbering starts again on each page, at each section or
runs on through the document, what number it starts at, and whether
every line shows its number or only every 5th or 10th (5, 10, 15...).
With newspaper columns, each column has its numbers on its own left.

To number the lines of a section, put the caret in it, choose
Format > Layout... and click Line numbers... The Line numbers box
opens with the section's numbering:

  Add line numbering      on: the section's lines are numbered; off:
                          no numbers (the other values are shaded)
  Start at                the first number (1 to 32767)
  Count by                1 shows every line's number, 5 every
                          fifth (5, 10, 15...) and so on (1 to 100)
  From text               how far the numbers are from the text (0
                          to 22 inches); Auto is Word's quarter of
                          an inch
  Numbering               Restart each page, Restart each section
                          or Continuous (on through the document)

Click OK (or press Return): the numbers change at once, and Undo
takes the change back in one step. The first time a document gets
line numbers, !Letters adds Word's Line Number style to it, so you
can change the numbers' font later in Word (a document saved with
no styles at all, which is rare, is left without one). A Count by
of 0, a start under 1, an empty field or text that is not a number
(such as 1.2.3) makes the bell sound and the box say why; nothing
changes, not even the other values, until they are right. A Count
by over 100 that a document already has is kept and used. Numbering
a document has written in a way !Letters does not understand shows
blank values: it is kept as it is, and can only be switched off.
The Layout box stays open behind, and its own OK still works.

To leave a paragraph out, put the caret in it, choose Format >
Paragraph..., click Suppress line numbers and then OK (one undo
step).

Floating pictures and text wrap
-------------------------------

A picture can sit in its line like a letter ("In line with text"),
or float: then the document gives its place on the page (so far
from the margin, the page's edge, its paragraph or its line) and
says how the text goes round it. In Print layout !Letters puts each
floating picture where the document puts it and lays the text out
round it:

  Square           the text stops at a box round the picture (the
                   picture plus the room the document keeps round
                   it) and goes on beside it
  Tight, Through   the text follows the picture's own outline (the
                   shape Word keeps for it), so it comes closer to
                   a round or slanted picture
  Top and bottom   no text beside the picture: the text stops
                   above it and goes on below it
  Behind text      the picture is drawn under the text, which runs
                   over it as if it were not there
  In front of text the picture is drawn over the text, which runs
                   under it

The text goes on one side of a picture only: the wider one, or the
side the document asks for when it says left only or right only
(Word's Wrap text: Left only, Right only). Word may put text on
both sides of a picture in the middle of the column: still to be
checked in real Word. Where less than half an inch is
left beside a picture no text goes there: the line moves down below
the picture. Each line is fitted at its own height, so a tall line
(one holding a picture in the text, say) does not run under a
float. A picture whose place would be off the foot of the page is
moved up so that it ends at the foot, and one above the top down to
it (still to be checked in real Word).

A floating picture anchored in a table's cell makes the text of that
cell flow round it, inside the cell; the text outside the table is
not moved. A picture that reaches across two newspaper columns
makes the text of both flow round it.

A click on a picture in front of the text selects it. A click on a
picture behind the text puts the caret in the text over it, as in
Word; click where the picture has no text over it to select it (or
SHIFT-Right or SHIFT-Left over its anchor, the place in its
paragraph where it is kept). A selected floating picture is resized
by its handles and, in Print layout, moved by dragging it (section
9): the text flows round it again where you let it go.

Text that flows round a picture can move paragraphs to the next
page, which can move the picture too. !Letters settles each page in
turn: the page with the caret at once, the pages after it a moment
later (in a long document with many floating pictures the pages far
below may change for a second or two after you type). To settle a
page it may keep a paragraph whole in the next column or page, or,
rarely, leave a line under a picture rather than go round in
circles; where Word lays a page out otherwise, Word's page is the
one that counts. The first line of a paragraph at the top of a
page stays there even under a picture too tall to go below on that
page.

Draft, Web layout and Outline show every floating picture in its
paragraph, with a dashed outline, and no text flows round it there.
So does Print layout for a floating picture in a header or footer,
and for one whose place the document gives in a way !Letters does
not understand: these are kept exactly as they are.

Format > Wrap text
------------------

Select a picture, then choose Format > Wrap text (after Picture...;
shaded when no picture is selected). Its menu ticks the kind the
picture has:

  In line with text  the picture sits in its line, like a letter
  Square, Tight, Top and bottom   as above
  Behind text, In front of text   as above

There is no Through item: a picture that has Through (made in Word)
is shown as Tight, and the menu ticks nothing for it.

Each choice is one step for CTRL-Z; choosing the kind the picture
already has changes nothing. A picture in the text made to float
stays where it was shown: its place across is where it stood in
its line (from the edge of its column) and its place down is where
it stood below the top of its paragraph (its line's offset kept, as
Word does), with an eighth of an inch kept clear to its left
and right, Word's own default. Choosing In line with text puts a
floating picture back in its line where it is anchored, keeping
its size and its alt text. Between the floating kinds only the way
the text goes round changes, not the picture's place. A floating
picture whose place !Letters does not understand beeps and is left
as it is. What Word makes of a picture changed here is still to be
checked in real Word.

Floating tables
---------------

A table can float too (in Word, Table Properties, Text wrapping:
Around). In Print layout !Letters places a floating table where the
document puts it, as for a picture, and the text flows round it on
the wider side, keeping the room the table asks for between it and
the text; less than half an inch beside it and the line moves down
below it. A table placed under the text it follows moves with that
text.

Inside a floating table everything works as in any other table:
click in it and type, Tab and SHIFT-Tab, the arrows (Up and Down go
in and out of it in the order of the document), every Table command,
copy and paste, Undo. As you type in it and it grows, the text
beside it flows round it again. Its column and row edges, its
right edge and its last row's bottom can be dragged as in any table
(section 10); the table's place on the page stays where the
document puts it: it cannot be moved by dragging.

Two floating tables may overlap, as in Word, unless one of them does
not allow it (Word's Table Properties, Positioning, Allow overlap
off) or both would sit at exactly the same place: then the one that
would sit on top of the other is put just below it. A floating
table that would end
below the foot of the page goes to the next page, with the text
after it. A Split table (or a copy pasted in the same document) of a
floating table is marked so that it never sits over the table it
came from.

A floating table is shown in the text, where it stands in the
document, with a dashed outline, in Draft, Web layout and Outline,
and also in Print layout when it is in a table's cell, in a header
or footer, taller than the page's room for text, or written in a
way !Letters does not understand. Its place, as the document gives
it, is always kept and saved.

Columns, line numbers and wrap in the other views
-------------------------------------------------

Draft and Outline show a section in columns as one column, at the
width of its first column; Web layout shows every section as one
column as wide as the window. Line numbers, and text flowing round
floating pictures and tables, are in Print layout only, as in Word.

Still to be checked in real Word: where Word puts a floating table
whose document gives no anchor, text on both sides of a picture or
table, a page that Word lays out once and keeps, and the many
smaller choices listed in the hand-off questions (the developers'
notes have them).


14) Saving
----------

Click MENU over a document's window. The menu has three ways to
save; each writes a Word document, and double-clicking it opens it
in !Letters again (or in Microsoft Word, if you take it to another
computer). Two have keys, shown on the menu, which work while the
document's window has the caret:

  CTRL-S        Save
  F3            the Save as box, by the caret, as in !Edit and
                !Draw: Escape or a click elsewhere closes it. For
                a new document that has never been saved, F3 is
                Save (the box opens in the middle of the screen).
                SHIFT-F3 is not Save as: it changes case (section 5)

  Save          writes the document back to the file it came from.
                The * after the name in the title goes. A new
                document that has never been saved opens the Save
                box instead (Save as, below).
  Save as       a Save box: drag its icon to a directory display,
                or click OK. The document is saved there, and the
                window takes the new name (the file it came from is
                left as it was).
  Save a copy   a Save box, the same way, for a copy: the window
                keeps its name and its *, as the document itself
                is not saved.

The Save box's OK button needs a full pathname (with a disc name or
a '.' in it, such as RAM::RamDisc0.$.Letter): a bare name only
works by dragging the icon. So the box is filled in for you: with
the document's own pathname, or for a new document with the
directory of the document you last opened or saved (the hard disc,
$, if there is none) and a name no file there has yet (Untitled,
or Untitled2, Untitled3... if that is taken). Edit the name and
click OK, or press Return.

If OK would save over a file that is already there (other than the
document's own file), a box asks first: '...' already exists.
Replace it? Replace saves over it; Cancel (and Return or Escape,
the safe answer) leaves the file and the document as they were.
Save a copy asks this even over the document's own file. Dragging
the Save box's icon to a directory display replaces a file of the
same name there without asking, as everywhere on the desktop.
There is no backup copy and no automatic saving: save as you go.

What is saved is the document as it is now, with every change you
made. If you go on typing while it is being saved, the * stays, for
those changes are not in the file; undo back to where you saved and
it goes. If an input method (for Japanese, say) is still composing
when you save, its text is put into the document first, and saved
with it.

A file that is locked, on a disc that cannot be written to, or with
a name a file cannot have is not saved: an error box says why and
the document keeps its *. A document cannot be saved over the file
of another document that is open in !Letters (nor can a copy): close
that one first.

On a HostFS drive (a folder of your own computer) the file reaches
the computer's folder a moment after Save. If the computer refuses
it (the folder is read-only, say), the HostFS Filer reports it, but
the document already shows as saved: save it again elsewhere.

File names. A Word document is file type &A7E (MSWordX in a
directory display). On the desktop a '.' separates directories, so
the name a file has on a Windows or Mac computer, Report.docx, is
written Report/docx here: !Letters saves a file under whatever name
you give it, with or without the /docx. On a HostFS drive (a folder
of your own computer, see the HostFS guide) Report/docx becomes
Report.docx in the folder, and a name without an extension, such as
Report, becomes Report,a7e: the ",a7e" keeps the file type. Rename
it to Report.docx to open it in Microsoft Word.

Revert, under Save as, loads the document again from its file, as
it was last saved. If it has changes, !Letters asks first: Discard
throws them away and reloads it, Cancel leaves it alone. The window
stays where it is, at the same size and zoom; undo starts afresh.
Revert is shaded for a new document that has never been saved. If
the file has gone, or is no longer a Word document, an error box
says so and the document is left as it was.


15) New documents, closing and quitting
---------------------------------------

New documents: click SELECT on !Letters' icon on the icon bar, or
choose New on its menu or on a document's menu. A new window opens
with a whole empty A4 page and the caret at its top: type straight
away. The window is as tall as the page where the screen has room
for it above the icon bar; scroll down to see the rest of the page.
Closing a new document you have not typed in asks nothing.

The keys, as in !Edit and !Draw, while a document's window has the
caret (the menus show them):

  F2            a new document (CTRL-N does the same, though the
                web browser may keep CTRL-N for itself)
  CTRL-F2       closes the window, as Close below (asking first if
                it has changes)
  CTRL-F10      puts the window at the back, behind the others

A key held down acts once: holding F2 makes one new document, not
one each time the key repeats; the same for CTRL-N, CTRL-F2, F3 and
CTRL-S. F4 opens the Find box, as CTRL-F does (section 4); the
other function keys (F1, F5, F6, F7, F10, F11, SHIFT-F2 and so on)
do nothing in a document, and F12 is the desktop's.

The first new document is called Untitled, the next Untitled 2,
then Untitled 3, and so on (the smallest number not in use). A file
name cannot have a space in it, so the Save box offers Untitled2
for the window Untitled 2; once saved, the window takes the name of
its file.

One click on the icon makes one document, and so does a
double-click: a second click within half a second of the one that
made a document is taken as part of the same double-click, so two
quick deliberate clicks also give one document. Wait a moment
between clicks for two.

Close, on a document's menu, CTRL-F2, or the window's close icon,
closes the window. If the document has changes that are not saved, a
box asks what to do with them:

  Save          saves it (as Save above), then closes the window.
                A new document opens the Save box first: the window
                closes once it has been saved, and stays open if
                the Save box is closed without saving. If it cannot
                be saved (a locked file, say), or you typed into it
                while it was being saved, the window stays open.
  Discard       closes the window; the changes are lost and the
                file is left as it was.
  Cancel        leaves the window open, with its changes.

Return is Save; Escape, or the box's close icon, is Cancel. Only one
such box is open at a time: closing another window while it is open
brings the box to the front (with a beep) instead of opening a
second one.

Quit on the icon bar menu stops !Letters. If any documents have changes
that are not saved, it asks once, saying how many (counted when the
box opens):

  Discard       throws the changes away and quits.
  Cancel        quits nothing: every window stays as it was.

Return is Discard, as in !Edit; Escape, or the box's close icon, is
Cancel. To keep the changes, Cancel, save the documents and quit
again. If a window's Save / Discard / Cancel box is already open,
that one is answered first; nothing more is asked if no changes are
left after it.

The same box appears when the Task Manager quits !Letters (Quit on its
menu over !Letters), when you shut down (Shutdown on the Task Manager's
menu, or CTRL-SHIFT-F12), and when you leave the desktop (Exit):

  - Task Manager Quit: Discard quits !Letters, Cancel keeps it.
  - Shutdown: Discard lets the shutdown go on (every program quits
    and the box to restart appears); Cancel stops it.
  - Exit: Discard also goes on to the shutdown, not to the command
    line, as with !Edit; Cancel stops it. To leave the desktop with
    nothing asked, save or close the documents first.

Recent, on the icon bar menu, lists the last 8 documents you opened
or saved, newest first; choose one to open it (if it is open, its
window comes to the front). The list is kept in Choices:Letters (the
file Letters in $.!Boot.Choices), so it is still there the next time
!Letters starts. A file that has been deleted or moved is left out. If
Choices:Letters itself is damaged, the list simply starts empty again.
(!Letters was called !Word before; the list it kept then, in
Choices:Word, is not read, so the list starts empty once.)

Not yet possible: automatic saving or a backup copy of the file
before it is saved over.


16) What is kept
----------------

Everything. The program understands the text, the paragraphs and the
formatting that it shows. Everything else in the file is kept exactly
as it was and written back when you save:

  - drawings that are not pictures, text boxes, charts
  - tables: every table is kept, and is saved as it was. Nearly all
    are read into the document's structure (rows, cells, widths,
    merged cells and what is in them) and drawn (section 10); a
    floating table's place is saved as the document gave it, and
    the settings that are not shown (text that runs up or down)
    are kept. One with
    tracked changes in the table, content controls round its rows
    or cells, a cell that ends with another table, or something
    else not understood is kept exactly as it was and shown as a
    grey box
  - pictures (one you resize, move or describe changes only in its
    size, place and alt text; one you delete leaves its file
    behind: section 9)
  - footnotes and endnotes
  - comments and tracked changes (insertions and deletions)
  - headers and footers: one you have not changed is saved exactly
    as it was; one !Letters cannot read is kept as it was and shown
    as a grey band (section 12)
  - fields: the code and the result in the file are kept; on the
    screen a page number or a date is worked out as you look, but
    the file keeps its old result until Edit > Update field (saving
    never updates a field)
  - links, bookmarks, content controls, equations
  - styles, numbered and bulleted lists, themes, settings
  - document properties (title, author...) and custom XML
  - fonts that are embedded in the file

How well does it do this? The reading and saving were tried on over
600 real Word documents, from several collections of sample files
with open licences, some very odd ones among them: after reading and
saving each one, all the text, formatting and other parts were found
to be the same as in the original. What was not possible during the
work is to try the copies in Microsoft Word itself, which was not
available to the people who wrote this program. The copies are
written to the rules Word's file format sets, and checked against
the format's published schema, but if one of your documents does not
open properly in Word after being saved by !Letters, please keep both
files: the original and the copy tell what went wrong.

Small differences, which do not change a document's content:
the order of some formatting items inside a paragraph is put in the
order Word requires; a few empty formatting items are left out;
the file is packed again, so it is not the same size; and if the
file's list of what it holds gives a part such as the footnotes or
the document properties only a general type ("application/xml"),
the copy gives that part its proper type. Without it Microsoft Word
cannot open a file whose document properties are typed so, and for
the footnotes it offers to recover the file after a message about
unreadable content.


17) Fonts
---------

Word documents mostly use Calibri, Cambria, Arial, Times New Roman
and Courier New. Those fonts belong to Microsoft and cannot be put on
this disc. !Letters shows them in fonts with the same letter widths, so
that lines end in the same places as they do in Word:

  Calibri          is shown in Carlito
  Cambria          is shown in Caladea
  Arial            is shown in Liberation Sans
  Times New Roman  is shown in Liberation Serif
  Courier New      is shown in Liberation Mono

Any other font is shown in that font if this computer has it,
otherwise in Liberation Sans. The font files are in the Fonts
directory inside !Letters (hold SHIFT and double-click !Letters to see
inside it), and are loaded when a document needs them: a document is
shown as soon as its fonts are loaded (at most a few seconds). Fonts
are loaded once, and stay ready for the next document.

Line heights come from the fonts
--------------------------------

How far apart the lines are depends on the font, as it does in
Word: each font file says how tall its letters reach above and
below the line, and !Letters uses that (single line spacing is one of
these heights; 1.5 lines is one and a half of them, and so on). For
the fonts above, a single-spaced line is:

  Calibri (Carlito)              about 1.22 times the font size
  Cambria (Caladea)              about 1.17 times
  Arial (Liberation Sans)        about 1.15 times
  Times New Roman (Liberation
    Serif)                       about 1.15 times
  Courier New (Liberation Mono)  about 1.13 times

the same as in Word. Before, every font's lines were 1.25 times its
size, so lines are now a little closer together than they were: a
line of 11-point Cambria is about 17.2 pixels high at zoom 100%
where it was 18.3 (Calibri 17.9, Arial 16.9), and more lines fit on
the screen and on a page, as in Word. Cambria's stand-in, Caladea,
has taller lines of its own than Cambria; !Letters uses Cambria's
1.17 for it so that lines fall where Word puts them. Any other font
keeps 1.25 times its size. A line that mixes fonts is as high as
its tallest letters above the line plus its deepest below it,
whichever fonts those are, as in Word. Exactly and At least line spacing
(section 6) are unchanged: they are the heights you give. A page
holds as many lines as fit, so these heights also decide where the
pages break (section 11): with them, !Letters' pages break nearer to
where Word's do.

All of these fonts are free: they are licensed under the SIL Open
Font License, version 1.1, which lets anyone use and pass them on.
They come from the Carlito, Caladea and Liberation fonts projects.
The licence texts are in the file Licences in the Fonts directory.


18) What it will not open
-------------------------

!Letters opens .docx files only. When it cannot read a file it says so
in a message box, naming the file, and carries on:

  'Old.doc' is an older Word (.doc) or password-protected file;
  !Letters opens .docx files only.

Older .doc files (made by Word 2003 and before) and documents saved
with a password are of a different kind, and are not read. In Word,
Save As .docx (and remove the password) to make a file that !Letters can
open. Other messages:

  it is not a Word .docx file       some other kind of file
  it has no main document in it     a zip file, but not a Word one
  part of it is damaged             the file is broken
  it is too big, encrypted or ...   see below
  it has more than 200,000 ...      see below

!Letters also refuses a file that would need an unreasonable amount of
memory, as some damaged or malicious files do: one that unpacks to
more than 256MB, or whose packing is more than a thousand times
smaller than the contents (the message says it is too big), or
that has more than 200,000 paragraphs, counting those in the cells
of tables (the message says so). Real documents are nowhere near
these limits.


19) Safety and privacy
----------------------

  - A document never runs anything. Nothing in the file is carried
    out, and a link is not followed. Fields are never run: !Letters
    works out only page numbers, the number of pages and sections,
    dates and times, the document's own properties (title, author,
    file name, word count), from the document and the clock; every
    other field is kept and shown as the file has it (section 12).
  - An INCLUDETEXT or INCLUDEPICTURE field, which in Word brings in
    another file or a picture from the web, is never fetched or
    updated, and a DDE or mail merge field never reaches another
    program or file.
  - The addresses you give a link are only text: only http, https,
    mailto and ftp are accepted, and !Letters never opens one or asks
    your browser to (section 8).
  - Nothing is fetched from the web. A document that refers to a
    picture or a template on a web site is shown without it (a
    grey box for a picture) and is saved with the reference as it
    was. Insert > Picture... takes a file of the disc only.
  - Opening a document does not change it, and the file is only read.
  - !Letters does not send your documents anywhere.


20) What is coming
------------------

!Letters is being built in steps. The first gave a sure footing: reading
and saving Word documents without losing anything. Then came
typing, deleting and undo; formatting from the keys, the Format
menu, a toolbar and a ruler, and zoom; the numbers and bullets of
lists, and changing an item's level; documents: Save, Save as and
Revert, new documents, recent files, and asking before changes are
thrown away; copy, cut and paste, and find and replace; the spacing
of paragraphs; making new bulleted and numbered lists; page and
section breaks and the Insert menu; tab stops; borders and shading,
symbols and special characters, and changing case; hyperlinks,
bookmarks, the format painter, a word count, pictures and tables;
pages: Print layout and Draft, Page setup and Layout, and pages
broken by Word's rules (section 11); headers, footers, page numbers
and fields, shown on the pages and changed (section 12); and now
columns side by side, line numbers, text that flows round pictures
and floating tables (section 13), and the Web layout and Outline
views (section 2). Later steps will add:

  - pages still closer to Word's (section 11 says how close they
    are today)
  - showing a link's ScreenTip; and links between documents (today
    a link is never opened, which is deliberate: section 19)
  - making and changing styles, bullets and numbering of your own
  - printing
  - pictures that you can turn, crop and give effects, and
    pictures in footnotes (section 9 has the list)
  - pasting lists from other programs as lists (tables come as
    tables: section 10)
  - more for tables (section 10 lists what is not possible yet):
    tracked changes to tables, editing table styles, AutoFit,
    sorting and formulas
  - footnotes at the foot of their page, and footnotes you can
    change
  - a table of contents made and updated from the headings (today
    one from Word is shown and kept as it is, not updated)
  - tracked changes: shown as Word marks them, accepted and
    rejected, and Track changes for your own edits
  - floating pictures and tables in headers and footers placed on
    the page, and text on both sides of a floating picture
  - saving as RTF and PDF
  - a spelling checker


21) For programmers: the source and WimpLib
-------------------------------------------

!Letters' source is in the application: open the !Letters directory
(SHIFT-double-click) and open any file in !JsEdit. The files have
no extension and are of file type JSScript. Start with !RunImage, the
file that starts the program; DocxRead and DocxWrite read and write
Word files; Model and Ops are the document and the changes made to
it.

Some of the program is a library that any program on the disc can
use: WimpLib. It lives with the system resources, in !WimpLib in the
Resources directory of !Boot ($.!Boot.Resources.!WimpLib). To see it,
hold SHIFT and double-click !Boot, then open Resources.
Double-clicking !WimpLib shows its help text, which lists its
modules.

Its files are plain JavaScript modules, imported with a name that
starts "wimplib/", wherever the program is:

  import {readZip, writeZip} from 'wimplib/Zip';
  import {parseXml, serialize, el} from 'wimplib/Xml';

A name after "wimplib/" is made of letters, digits, _ and - (not
starting with -), with / between directories: 'wimplib/Zip', not
'wimplib/Zip.js'. Any other name is refused, so an import can only
reach the library. "wimplib" itself can be in any case.

When the desktop starts, the library's !Boot file sets two system
variables (to see them, press F12 for the command line and type
*Show WimpLib$*):

  WimpLib$Dir    the library's directory
  WimpLib$Path   the directories a program looks in for a module,
                 in order, with commas between them; normally just
                 <WimpLib$Dir>.

A program finds 'wimplib/Zip' in the first directory of WimpLib$Path
that has a Zip in it, and in WimpLib$Dir last. In an Obey file or at
the command line, the same file is <WimpLib$Dir>.Zip.

To try a changed copy of a module without changing the library, put
it in a directory of your own and put that directory first. Do it
after the desktop has started (this desktop runs no boot file of
yours before the library's !Boot), at the command line or in an
Obey file:

  *Set WimpLib$Path RAM::RamDisc0.$.MyLib.,<WimpLib$Dir>.

Programs started afterwards use your copy of that module. Copy a
module together with the files it imports: Zip, for one, only
re-exports ZipRead and ZipWrite, so a lone copy of Zip cannot find
"./ZipError". The library's own modules import each other by
relative names, so a changed inner module (ZipRead, say) is not seen
by the other library modules that import it, only by programs that
import it by name.

WimpLib$Path is stored with <WimpLib$Dir> already filled in, so
changing WimpLib$Dir later does nothing until WimpLib$Path is set
again. A name with the prefix in any case is found on the disc
(WimpLib/Zip works); the files of the source tree, run in Node, only
accept lower case "wimplib/".

Only programs that run JavaScript read these two variables, and only
a *Set (the command line, an Obey file or an application's !Boot)
changes them. Showing a directory in a Filer window runs the !Boot of
each application in it, so treat a downloaded application directory
as you would treat running it.

Today it has Zip (and ZipRead, ZipWrite, ZipError, ZipNames, Crc32)
for reading and writing zip files, Xml (XmlParse, XmlText, XmlWrite)
for reading and writing XML safely, Segment and TextMetrics for
text, FontList, NumberField, ColourList, CharSets (named sets of
characters for a symbol picker), RulerMath, RulerPick (which ruler
marker a click picks) and RulerTabs (a ruler's tab stops: adding,
dragging, the tab-type selector), Units (lengths typed as 0.5" or
36 pt), DialogLayout, DialogIcons and DialogValues (the parts of
Ui.Dialog that need no desktop), and in its directory Ui the parts
of !Letters' windows that other programs can use too: Ui.Toolbar (a
row of buttons as a pane across a window's top, with toggles, radio
groups, popup arrows and fields), Ui.ColourPopup (a box of swatches
opened as a menu), Ui.FontMenu, Ui.Ruler (a ruler in inches with
markers and tab stops to drag; Ui.RulerPaint and Ui.RulerTabPaint
draw it), Ui.PaneWheel (a pane's mouse wheel scrolls its window),
Ui.SaveQuery (the Save / Discard / Cancel box), Ui.FindBox (a Find
and Replace box: the program does the finding), Ui.CharGrid (a
window of characters to pick from, as Insert > Symbol uses; its
drawing is Ui.CharGridPaint) and Ui.Dialog (a dialogue box made
from rows of fields, option and radio icons, popup menus and colour
buttons, with OK and Cancel), imported as 'wimplib/Ui/Toolbar' and
so on.
The programming books in Manuals (JSApps) show how a program is
made; the developers' notes for !Letters are in docs/apps/Letters.md in
the source of this desktop.


22) Things to know
------------------

  - The hard disc is kept in your web browser's storage (see the
    README). Clearing the browser's data for this site removes the
    documents you saved there with everything else.
  - There is no Print yet; MENU over a document shows what there
    is.
  - !Letters does not save on its own and keeps no backup: Save
    writes over the file. Save often, and use Save a copy to keep
    an older version.
  - Pages are made by Word's rules, but they are not always Word's:
    a page may start in another place than Word's, once one page
    differs the pages after it usually do too, and the number of
    pages can differ. Footnotes are not on the pages yet (headers,
    footers, columns and floating pictures and tables are:
    sections 12 and 13). Section 11 says how
    close they are; where they differ, Word's pages are the ones
    that count.
  - Links are never opened, only edited, removed, or (to a
    bookmark) followed with CTRL-click.
  - A page number or date shown on the screen is worked out as you
    look; the file keeps the result it had until Edit > Update field
    (section 12). F9 is Redo, as in !Edit, not Word's Update field.
  - Link to previous, in the Header menu, deletes the section's own
    header without asking first (Word asks); CTRL-Z puts it back.
  - You can make tables, add and delete rows and columns, merge and
    split cells, type, select and format in cells, change a
    table's width, lines and shading, copy and paste cells, rows
    and tables, and convert text to a table and back. How a
    table's lines, widths and style colours compare with Word's is
    still to be checked (section 10).
  - Floating pictures and tables are placed on their pages, with
    the text flowing round them, in Print layout only; the other
    views show them in the text with a dashed outline, and so do
    headers and footers in every view (sections 9, 10 and 13).
  - !Letters' behaviour in a few places is a careful guess at what Word
    does, not something seen: the parts of this guide that describe
    them say "still to be checked in real Word".
    The main ones are how Word lays out and counts the same document
    (line breaks, pages, the word count), what Word does with
    bookmarks when text beside them is deleted, the format painter,
    and the way lists, tab stops, borders and a few special
    characters are drawn.
    Where a document looks different in Word, Word is right.
